window.BOOKS=[];function B(b){window.BOOKS.push(b)}
B({id:"atomic-habits",title:"Atomic Habits",author:"James Clear",year:2018,topic:"Productivity",color:"#c2532b",
big:"Tiny changes, repeated, compound into remarkable results, so build systems and an identity, not just goals.",
model:"The habit loop: Cue → Craving → Response → Reward. Four laws to build a habit: make it Obvious, Attractive, Easy, Satisfying. Invert them to break one: Invisible, Unattractive, Difficult, Unsatisfying.",
points:["Improving 1% a day compounds; results lag behind effort (the 'plateau of latent potential').","Goals set direction; systems make progress.","Lasting change starts with identity: every action is a vote for who you want to be.","Every habit runs on a loop: cue, craving, response, reward.","Use implementation intentions and habit stacking to make cues obvious.","Design your surroundings so the good choice is the easy one.","Shrink new habits to two minutes; show up before you scale up.","Track habits and never miss twice."],
s:[
{t:"Why small habits matter",c:[
["The maths of 1% better","If you get 1% better each day for a year, you end up about 37 times better. Get 1% worse each day and you decline to nearly zero. Habits are the compound interest of self-improvement: tiny daily choices that feel meaningless add up to huge differences over time.","Pick one area where a 1% daily gain would matter most to you."],
["The plateau of latent potential","Progress is rarely a straight line. Like an ice cube that doesn't melt until the room passes 0°C, effort builds up out of sight before results appear. Clear calls the frustrating gap the 'valley of disappointment'. Most people quit there, just before the breakthrough.","Think of a habit you dropped because 'nothing was happening'."],
["Forget goals, focus on systems","Winners and losers often share the same goal, so goals alone can't explain success. Goals are about results; systems are the daily process that produces them. A goal is also a moment: once reached, it stops pushing you. A system keeps you improving.","Rewrite one goal as a daily system, e.g. 'ride 3 times a week' instead of 'win a race'."],
["Identity: the deepest layer","Change can happen at three levels: outcomes (what you get), processes (what you do) and identity (what you believe about yourself). Most people start with outcomes. Clear says start with identity. 'I'm trying to quit smoking' and 'I'm not a smoker' lead to very different choices.","Finish the sentence: 'I'm the kind of person who…'"],
["Every action is a vote","Each time you act, you cast a vote for the type of person you're becoming. You don't need a unanimous vote, just a majority. One workout doesn't make you fit, but it's evidence that you're someone who trains. Small wins build belief, and belief keeps the habit going.","Name one small 'vote' you could cast today."]],
q:[["Roughly how much better do you get after a year of 1% daily improvement?",["About 3.7 times","About 37 times","About 365 times","About 1% better"],1,"1.01 to the power of 365 is roughly 37: small gains compound."],
["What does Clear call the period when effort hasn't shown results yet?",["The comfort zone","The valley of disappointment","The habit gap","The learning curve"],1,"Results lag behind effort; many people quit in this valley."],
["Which of the three layers of change does Clear say to start with?",["Outcomes","Processes","Identity","Goals"],2,"Lasting habits come from changing what you believe about yourself."]],
x:["In your own words: why does Clear say systems matter more than goals?","Goals only set a direction, and winners and losers often share the same goals. What makes the difference is the daily system. Goals are also temporary: once you hit one, the motivation stops. A system keeps you improving, and it's what you actually control day to day."]},
{t:"Make it obvious and attractive",c:[
["The habit loop","Every habit follows four steps. A cue triggers a craving, which motivates a response (the habit itself), which delivers a reward. The reward teaches your brain to repeat the loop next time the cue appears. The four laws of behaviour change map onto these four steps.","Pick one habit and name its cue, craving, response and reward."],
["Implementation intentions","Vague plans fail. A clear plan does much better: 'I will [behaviour] at [time] in [location].' Studies Clear cites found people who wrote down when and where they'd exercise were far more likely to do it. Clarity beats motivation.","Write one line: 'I will ___ at ___ in ___.'"],
["Habit stacking","Link a new habit to one you already do: 'After I [current habit], I will [new habit].' Your existing routine becomes the cue. For example, 'After I pour my morning coffee, I'll read one Daily Ideas card.'","Choose an existing habit to stack a new one onto."],
["Design your environment","People often choose things because of where they are, not what they are. Clear describes a hospital café that put water in more fridges and by the tills: water sales rose and fizzy drink sales fell, with no willpower needed. Make good cues visible and bad ones invisible. One space, one use helps too.","Move one temptation out of sight and put one good cue in plain view."],
["Make it attractive","We act on the anticipation of reward. Temptation bundling pairs something you need to do with something you want to do, like only watching your favourite show while on the exercise bike. Joining a group where your desired behaviour is normal also makes it more attractive.","Pair one 'should do' with one 'want to do' this week."]],
q:[["What are the four steps of the habit loop, in order?",["Cue, craving, response, reward","Goal, plan, action, review","Trigger, action, habit, result","Craving, cue, reward, response"],0,"The cue triggers a craving, which drives the response, which delivers the reward."],
["Which is an implementation intention?",["'I want to read more'","'I will read for 10 minutes at 9pm in the lounge'","'Reading is important'","'I'll read when I'm less busy'"],1,"It names the behaviour, time and place."],
["What is temptation bundling?",["Hiding temptations away","Pairing something you need to do with something you want to do","Giving in to temptations once a week","Bundling several habits together"],1,"Linking the 'should' with a 'want' makes the habit more attractive."]],
x:["Explain habit stacking and give an example you could use.","Habit stacking links a new habit to an existing one, so the old habit acts as the cue: 'After I [current habit], I will [new habit].' Example: 'After I pour my coffee, I'll do one Daily Ideas card.' It works because the cue is already reliable."]},
{t:"Make it easy and satisfying",c:[
["Repetitions, not time","Habits form through the number of repetitions, not how long you've been doing them. Clear tells of a photography class split in two: one group graded on quantity, one on quality. The quantity group, who kept shooting and learning, produced the best photos. Start doing, not planning.","Count repetitions of your habit this week, not days."],
["The law of least effort","We drift toward the option that takes the least effort. So reduce friction for good habits (lay your kit out the night before) and add friction to bad ones (unplug the TV, log out of apps). Small changes in effort make big changes in behaviour.","Remove one step between you and a good habit."],
["The two-minute rule","Scale any new habit down to a version that takes two minutes: 'read before bed' becomes 'read one page'. The point is to master the art of showing up. A habit must be established before it can be improved.","Shrink one habit you've been putting off to its two-minute version."],
["Make it satisfying now","What is rewarded is repeated, but good habits often pay off later while bad ones pay off now. So add an immediate reward. Clear describes a stockbroker who moved a paperclip from one jar to another after each sales call: a simple visible sign of progress.","Give yourself a small instant reward after your habit today."],
["Track it, and never miss twice","A habit tracker (like a streak) is satisfying and keeps you honest. When you slip, don't let one miss become two. Missing once is an accident; missing twice is the start of a new habit. The all-or-nothing mindset is what breaks habits.","Decide now what your 'never miss twice' rescue looks like."]],
q:[["According to Clear, what forms a habit?",["The amount of time","The number of repetitions","Willpower","Motivation"],1,"Repetitions, not time, wire in a habit."],
["What does the two-minute rule help you master?",["Speed","Showing up","Perfection","Planning"],1,"A habit must exist before it can be improved."],
["What's the rule when you slip up?",["Start again next month","Never miss twice","Double up the next day","Lower the goal"],1,"One miss is an accident; two starts a new habit."]],
x:["How would you use the four laws to break a bad habit of your own?","Invert them: make it invisible (remove the cue), unattractive (focus on its costs), difficult (add friction, like logging out or moving it away) and unsatisfying (an accountability partner or a cost for doing it). For example, to scroll less: keep the phone in another room, delete the app and tell someone your goal."]}]});

B({id:"deep-work",title:"Deep Work",author:"Cal Newport",year:2016,topic:"Productivity",color:"#2f5d7d",
big:"Focused, distraction-free work on hard problems is becoming both rarer and more valuable, so train for it and protect it.",
model:"High-quality work = time spent × intensity of focus. Four rules: Work deeply; Embrace boredom; Quit social media (or use it like a craftsman); Drain the shallows.",
points:["Deep work is focused effort that pushes your ability to its limit; shallow work is easy, interruptible admin.","Two core skills win in today's economy: learning hard things fast and producing at an elite level.","Switching tasks leaves 'attention residue' that drags your performance down.","Pick a depth philosophy that suits your life: monastic, bimodal, rhythmic or journalistic.","Rituals, routines and a shutdown ritual make depth repeatable.","Concentration is a muscle; constant distraction weakens it.","Judge tools by whether their benefits outweigh their costs to what matters most.","Time-block your day and keep shallow work in its place."],
s:[
{t:"The case for deep work",c:[
["Deep vs shallow","Deep work is professional activity done in distraction-free concentration that pushes your thinking to its limit. It creates new value and is hard to copy. Shallow work is logistical, often done while distracted: email, admin, quick replies. It's easy to copy and adds little.","Estimate how many hours of truly deep work you did last week."],
["Why it's valuable","Newport argues two abilities matter most in a fast-changing, tech-driven economy: learning hard things quickly, and producing at an elite level in quality and speed. Both depend on deep work.","Which hard skill would most change your work if you mastered it?"],
["Why it's rare","Open-plan offices, instant messaging and the pressure to be always available make deep work rare. Busyness feels like productivity, because without clear measures people default to looking busy.","Notice when you were 'busy' today but not productive."],
["Attention residue","Researcher Sophie Leroy found that when you switch tasks, part of your attention stays stuck on the old one. Even a quick inbox check leaves residue that lowers your performance on the next task for a while. Work in long, single-task blocks.","Try one hour today with email and messages fully closed."],
["The formula","Newport's formula: high-quality work produced = time spent × intensity of focus. Raise the intensity and you get more done in less time. A focused 2 hours can beat a distracted 6.","Pick one task to do at maximum intensity tomorrow."]],
q:[["What does 'attention residue' describe?",["Notes left from a meeting","Part of your attention staying on a previous task after switching","Tiredness at the end of the day","Unread emails"],1,"Switching leaves attention behind and lowers performance."],
["Newport's formula for high-quality work is time spent multiplied by what?",["Hours of sleep","Intensity of focus","Number of tasks","Experience"],1,"Increase intensity and output rises."],
["Which is an example of shallow work?",["Writing a strategy","Learning a new skill","Answering routine email","Designing a product"],2,"Shallow work is logistical, easy to replicate and often done while distracted."]],
x:["Explain why deep work is both rare and valuable.","It's valuable because the economy rewards people who can learn hard things quickly and produce excellent work, and both need deep focus. It's rare because modern workplaces push constant connectivity: open offices, messaging and the need to look busy. That combination makes it a competitive advantage."]},
{t:"Rule 1: work deeply",c:[
["Choose a depth philosophy","Newport offers four styles. Monastic: cut out almost all shallow work. Bimodal: alternate long deep stretches with normal periods (the psychologist Carl Jung retreated to a stone tower to write). Rhythmic: a fixed deep block every day. Journalistic: fit deep work in whenever a gap appears; this one is hardest for beginners.","Which of the four suits your life best?"],
["Ritualise it","Great deep workers use rituals: where they work, for how long, how they'll work (no internet, for example) and how they'll support it (coffee, a walk first). Rituals cut the willpower needed to get started.","Write a 3-line ritual: where, how long, what rules."],
["The grand gesture","Sometimes a big change of setting helps you commit. J.K. Rowling checked into an Edinburgh hotel suite to finish the last Harry Potter book. Investing time or money raises how seriously you take the work.","What's a small 'grand gesture' you could use for a big task?"],
["Execute like a business","Newport borrows the 4 Disciplines of Execution: focus on the wildly important; act on lead measures (like hours of deep work), not lag measures (like results); keep a visible scoreboard; and review it weekly.","Track your deep-work hours on paper for a week."],
["The shutdown ritual","End each workday with a set routine: check your inbox and tasks one last time, plan tomorrow, then say a fixed phrase (Newport says 'shutdown complete'). This tells your brain work is done, so it can rest and recover for tomorrow's focus.","Create your own two-minute shutdown routine today."]],
q:[["Which depth philosophy schedules a deep-work block at the same time every day?",["Monastic","Bimodal","Rhythmic","Journalistic"],2,"Rhythmic depth turns deep work into a simple daily habit."],
["What is a lead measure in Newport's approach?",["Results achieved","Hours spent in deep work","Money earned","Emails answered"],1,"Lead measures are the behaviours that drive results."],
["Why use a shutdown ritual?",["To check social media","To let your mind rest so it can recharge","To work later","To impress colleagues"],1,"Downtime restores the attention you need for deep work."]],
x:["Describe your own deep-work ritual using Newport's ideas.","A good answer names where you'll work, for how long, the rules (phone in another room, no email), what supports you (coffee, music, a walk first) and how you'll track it (hours on a scoreboard). It ends with a shutdown routine so work stays at work."]},
{t:"Rules 2-4: boredom, tools and the shallows",c:[
["Embrace boredom","If you reach for your phone at every quiet moment, your brain loses the ability to concentrate. Newport's advice: don't take breaks from distraction, take breaks from focus. Schedule set times for the internet and stay offline the rest of the time.","Next time you queue or wait, leave the phone in your pocket."],
["Productive meditation","Take a walk, run or drive and focus on one well-defined problem the whole time. When your mind wanders, bring it back. It turns 'dead time' into thinking time and trains concentration.","Pick one problem to think through on your next ride or walk."],
["The craftsman approach to tools","Most people adopt a tool if it has any benefit at all. Newport suggests keeping a tool only if its benefits clearly outweigh its costs for the things that matter most to you. Try a 30-day break from a social app, then ask whether you missed it and whether anyone noticed.","Choose one app for a 30-day break."],
["Schedule every minute","Time-block your workday on paper in 30-minute chunks. When plans change, and they will, simply redraw the rest of the day. The aim isn't rigidity; it's deciding on purpose what each hour is for.","Time-block tomorrow morning on paper."],
["Drain the shallows","Ask how long it would take to train a smart graduate to do a task: if not long, it's shallow. Set a budget for shallow work, finish by a fixed time each day, and be harder to reach. Replies that propose a clear process cut email back-and-forth.","Set an end time for your workday and stick to it this week."]],
q:[["What does Newport mean by 'take breaks from focus'?",["Rest every 20 minutes","Schedule internet use and stay focused otherwise","Never take breaks","Work only in the mornings"],1,"Distraction should be the scheduled exception, not the default."],
["How does the craftsman approach decide whether to use a tool?",["If it has any benefit","If everyone else uses it","If its benefits clearly outweigh its costs for what matters most","If it's free"],2,"It rejects the 'any benefit' test."],
["How can you tell if a task is shallow?",["It takes over an hour","A smart graduate could be trained to do it quickly","It involves writing","It's urgent"],1,"Shallow tasks need little training and add little new value."]],
x:["Which of Newport's four rules would make the biggest difference for you, and why?","A strong answer picks one rule (work deeply, embrace boredom, quit social media or drain the shallows), links it to a specific problem (e.g. constant email interruptions) and names one concrete change (e.g. two email blocks a day, phone in another room)."]}]});

B({id:"lean-startup",title:"The Lean Startup",author:"Eric Ries",year:2011,topic:"Business",color:"#7a4e9b",
big:"Treat a new venture as a series of experiments: build the smallest thing that tests your riskiest assumption, measure, learn and adapt fast.",
model:"Build → Measure → Learn, as fast as possible. Test leap-of-faith assumptions with an MVP, track actionable metrics, then decide: pivot or persevere.",
points:["A startup is an organisation creating something new under extreme uncertainty.","Progress is measured by validated learning, not features built.","Identify your leap-of-faith assumptions: the value hypothesis and the growth hypothesis.","An MVP is the least effort needed to test an assumption, not a cheap product.","Use actionable metrics and cohorts, not vanity metrics.","Pivot or persevere based on evidence, at regular checkpoints.","Work in small batches to find problems sooner.","Ask 'why?' five times to find the root cause of problems."],
s:[
{t:"Startups learn, they don't just build",c:[
["What a startup is","Ries defines a startup as a human institution designed to create a new product or service under conditions of extreme uncertainty. That includes a team inside a big company or a side project in your garage. Uncertainty, not size, is what matters.","Name the biggest uncertainty in your own idea."],
["The cost of building the wrong thing","Ries's company IMVU spent months building a product feature customers didn't want. The lesson: the biggest waste is building something nobody needs, however well it's made.","List what you'd build first, then ask: do I know anyone wants it?"],
["Validated learning","Progress for a startup is learning what customers really want, backed by evidence from real behaviour. Not opinions, and not how much you've built. 'We learned that 40% of visitors sign up' beats 'we built ten features'.","Write one thing you'd like to learn about your customers this month."],
["Leap-of-faith assumptions","Every plan rests on a few untested beliefs. Ries highlights two: the value hypothesis (will people get value from this?) and the growth hypothesis (how will new customers find it?). Test these first, because if they're wrong, nothing else matters.","Write your value hypothesis in one sentence."],
["Go and see","Borrowing from Toyota's 'genchi gembutsu', Ries says founders should go and see customers for themselves rather than rely on reports or assumptions. Real observation beats market research spreadsheets.","Plan to watch one potential customer deal with the problem you're solving."]],
q:[["How does Ries define a startup?",["Any company under 5 years old","An institution creating something new under extreme uncertainty","A tech company with investors","A small business"],1,"It's about uncertainty, not size or sector."],
["What are the two leap-of-faith assumptions?",["Price and cost","Value hypothesis and growth hypothesis","Market and competition","Product and team"],1,"Will it deliver value, and how will it grow?"],
["What counts as progress in a lean startup?",["Features shipped","Validated learning","Hours worked","Money raised"],1,"Evidence about what customers actually want."]],
x:["In your own words, what is validated learning and why does it matter?","Validated learning is evidence from real customer behaviour that shows whether your assumptions are right. It matters because building the wrong thing is the biggest waste; learning what people want first saves time and money."]},
{t:"MVPs and measuring the right things",c:[
["The minimum viable product","An MVP is the version of a product that lets you complete a full build-measure-learn loop with the least effort. It isn't a smaller version of your final product. It's an experiment. It can feel embarrassingly basic.","Describe the most basic test of your idea you could run in a week."],
["The Dropbox video","Before the product was ready, Dropbox's Drew Houston posted a short video showing how it would work. Its beta waiting list leapt from about 5,000 to 75,000 people overnight. The video tested demand without building the full product.","Could a video, mock-up or sketch test your idea?"],
["Fake the back end","Zappos founder Nick Swinmurn tested whether people would buy shoes online by photographing shoes in local shops and buying them at full price when an order came in. A 'concierge' MVP does the work by hand for a few customers before automating it.","What part of your idea could you do by hand first?"],
["Vanity vs actionable metrics","Vanity metrics like total sign-ups always go up and make you feel good, but don't guide decisions. Actionable metrics show cause and effect, such as what share of this month's new customers came back. Ries recommends cohort analysis: comparing groups who joined at different times.","Pick one number that would genuinely change your next decision."],
["Split tests","Show two versions to different customers at the same time and compare behaviour. It removes guesswork about what works. The test should answer a specific question about your assumptions.","Think of one A/B test you could run for your idea."]],
q:[["What is the main purpose of an MVP?",["To launch a cheaper product","To learn by testing an assumption with minimum effort","To impress investors","To beat competitors to market"],1,"It's an experiment, not a mini product."],
["What did the Dropbox video test?",["Server costs","Whether people wanted the product","The price","The design"],1,"Demand, before the full product existed."],
["Why are total sign-ups a vanity metric?",["They're hard to measure","They always rise and don't show cause and effect","They're private","They're too small"],1,"Actionable metrics link actions to results."]],
x:["Explain the difference between a vanity metric and an actionable metric, with an example.","A vanity metric looks good but doesn't guide decisions, like total downloads, which only goes up. An actionable metric shows cause and effect, like the percentage of new users from a specific campaign who are still active after a month. That tells you whether a change worked."]},
{t:"Pivot, persevere and grow",c:[
["Innovation accounting","Ries suggests three steps: use an MVP to set a baseline (where you are now), tune the engine (try changes to improve the numbers), then decide whether to pivot or persevere. It turns vague progress into something measurable.","Write down one baseline number for your idea."],
["Pivot or persevere","A pivot is a structured change of course that keeps what you've learned. Examples include zooming in on one feature, targeting a different customer segment or changing how you make money. Set regular checkpoints to decide, so you don't drift for years.","Set a date to review your evidence honestly."],
["Engines of growth","Ries describes three ways startups grow. Sticky: keep customers so they stay. Viral: customers bring in more customers as a side effect of using it. Paid: spend money on ads where each customer is worth more than they cost. Focus on one at a time.","Which engine fits your idea best?"],
["Small batches","Ries tells of stuffing envelopes: doing one envelope at a time from start to finish is faster than folding all the letters, then stuffing all, then sealing all. Small batches reveal problems sooner and cut waste.","Break your next project into the smallest complete piece."],
["The five whys","When something goes wrong, ask 'why?' five times to reach the root cause, which is often a human or process issue rather than a technical one. Then fix it in proportion to the damage.","Use the five whys on one recent problem."]],
q:[["What is a pivot?",["Giving up","A structured change of course that keeps what you've learned","A product launch","A price cut"],1,"It changes strategy while keeping the learning."],
["Which engine of growth relies on customers bringing in other customers?",["Sticky","Viral","Paid","Organic"],1,"Growth happens as a side effect of people using the product."],
["What are the 'five whys' for?",["Writing a mission","Finding the root cause of a problem","Pricing","Hiring"],1,"Keep asking why to reach the underlying cause."]],
x:["How could you use Lean Startup ideas on a product idea of your own?","Name the riskiest assumption (will people pay? do they have this problem?), design a quick MVP to test it (a video, landing page, pre-orders or doing it by hand), set one actionable metric and a checkpoint date, then decide to pivot or persevere based on what you learn."]}]});

B({id:"mom-test",title:"The Mom Test",author:"Rob Fitzpatrick",year:2013,topic:"Business",color:"#3d7a5a",
big:"People lie to be nice, so ask about their life and past behaviour, never your idea, and look for real commitment.",
model:"The Mom Test: talk about their life, not your idea; ask about specifics in the past, not opinions about the future; talk less, listen more. Then push for commitment: time, reputation or money.",
points:["Asking 'Do you like my idea?' gets polite lies, even from your mum.","Good questions are about the person's life, problems and current behaviour.","Past specifics beat future hypotheticals.","Compliments, fluff and feature requests are bad data: steer back to facts.","Dig into the 'why' behind requests and emotions.","Keep conversations casual; a coffee chat beats a formal meeting.","Real interest shows as commitment: time, reputation or money.","Prepare your three big questions and take notes; review them with your team."],
s:[
{t:"The three rules",c:[
["Everyone is lying (to be nice)","If you tell your mum your business idea, she'll say it's great because she loves you. Most people do the same: they protect your feelings. So asking whether people like your idea gives you useless, misleading data.","Remember the last time someone praised your idea. Did they buy anything?"],
["Rule 1: talk about their life","Instead of describing your idea, ask about the person's world: their problems, goals and how they handle things now. You learn what matters to them, and they can't flatter you because you haven't shown them anything.","Rewrite 'Would you use an app that…' as a question about their life."],
["Rule 2: past specifics, not future fluff","'Would you…?' and 'Do you ever…?' invite guesses and wishful thinking. 'Tell me about the last time that happened' gets facts. What people did is far more reliable than what they say they will do.","Ask someone: 'When did that last happen? What did you do?'"],
["Rule 3: talk less, listen more","The more you talk, the less you learn. Stop pitching. Ask, then let silence do its work. If you catch yourself explaining your idea, you've stopped learning.","In your next conversation, aim for them to talk 80% of the time."],
["Good questions to steal","Fitzpatrick suggests questions like: 'Why do you bother?', 'What are the implications of that?', 'Talk me through the last time that happened', 'What else have you tried?' and 'Where does the money come from?'","Pick two of these to use this week."]],
q:[["What does 'the Mom Test' protect you from?",["Bad pricing","People giving polite but misleading answers","Competitors","Legal risks"],1,"People lie to be nice, especially about your idea."],
["Which question passes the Mom Test?",["'Would you buy this?'","'Do you think it's a good idea?'","'Talk me through the last time this happened'","'How much would you pay for it?'"],2,"It asks about specific past behaviour."],
["What should you do with most of a customer conversation?",["Pitch","Listen","Negotiate","Take orders"],1,"Talk less, listen more."]],
x:["In your own words, what are the three rules of the Mom Test?","1) Talk about their life instead of your idea. 2) Ask about specifics in the past instead of generics or opinions about the future. 3) Talk less and listen more. Together they stop people being polite and get you real facts."]},
{t:"Avoiding bad data",c:[
["Compliments are noise","'That's brilliant, I'd love it!' feels great but proves nothing. When you get a compliment, deflect it and return to facts: 'Thanks. How are you dealing with this at the moment?'","Practise a one-line reply that turns a compliment back to facts."],
["Spot the fluff","Fluff comes in three types: generic claims ('I usually…', 'I always…'), future promises ('I would…', 'I will…') and hypotheticals ('I might…', 'I could…'). Anchor them: 'When did that last happen?'","Listen for 'would' and 'usually' in your next chat."],
["Dig beneath feature requests","When someone asks for a feature, don't just note it down. Ask why they want it, what it would let them do, and how they cope without it. The motivation behind a request is the valuable part.","Next time someone suggests a feature, ask 'What would that let you do?'"],
["Dig into emotions","If someone sounds angry, excited or frustrated about a problem, dig in: 'That seems to really bug you. Tell me more.' Strong emotions point to real problems worth solving.","Notice the strongest emotion in your next conversation."],
["Don't seek approval or pitch","If you hear yourself explaining what you're building, you've started pitching. People switch to being polite. Fitzpatrick also warns against 'Do you think…?' questions: you want facts about their life, not their opinion of your plan.","Notice when you start pitching, and stop."]],
q:[["How should you respond to a compliment about your idea?",["Thank them and move on happily","Deflect it and steer back to facts about their life","Ask them to buy","Write it down as proof"],1,"Compliments are noise; facts are signal."],
["Which phrase signals 'fluff'?",["'Last Tuesday I…'","'I would definitely…'","'We spent £200 on…'","'Here's what we use now'"],1,"Future promises and hypotheticals aren't reliable."],
["What should you do with a feature request?",["Build it immediately","Ignore it","Dig into the motivation behind it","Charge extra"],2,"The reason behind it is the useful insight."]],
x:["Give an example of a bad question and rewrite it to pass the Mom Test.","Bad: 'Would you buy a gadget that cleans your BBQ grill?' (invites polite guessing). Better: 'When did you last clean your BBQ grill? How did you do it? What was annoying about it? What have you tried to make it easier?' These ask about real past behaviour."]},
{t:"Commitment and running conversations",c:[
["Look for commitment","Real interest costs people something. Fitzpatrick names three currencies: time (a follow-up meeting, a trial), reputation (an introduction to their boss or a friend) and money (a pre-order, a deposit). If they won't give any, they aren't really interested.","End your next conversation by asking for a small commitment."],
["Beware zombie leads","Meetings that 'went well' but ended with no next step create zombie leads: people who stay friendly but never buy. A meeting that ends with no commitment hasn't succeeded, however nice it felt.","Look back: which 'good' conversations led nowhere?"],
["Keep it casual","Formal interviews put people on guard. A chat over coffee, or a few questions at an event, often gets more honest answers. Conversations can be 10 minutes long.","Think of a casual setting where you could ask your questions."],
["Prepare your big three","Before each conversation, write down the three most important things you want to learn. Good questions are sometimes scary because the answer could kill your idea; ask them anyway.","Write your three big questions for your current idea."],
["Who to talk to, and taking notes","Narrow down to a specific type of customer you can find and reach. Take notes in the moment, using simple symbols for emotions, problems and goals, and share them with anyone working with you. At the end, ask 'Who else should I talk to?'","Name one specific type of person to talk to first."]],
q:[["What are Fitzpatrick's three currencies of commitment?",["Time, reputation and money","Likes, shares and comments","Praise, interest and advice","Ideas, feedback and reviews"],0,"Real interest costs people something."],
["What is a 'zombie lead'?",["A customer who complains","A friendly contact who never commits or buys","A competitor","A lost email"],1,"Nice meetings with no next step create zombie leads."],
["What should you ask at the end of a good conversation?",["'So, do you like it?'","'Who else should I talk to?'","'Can I have a testimonial?'","'What's your budget?'"],1,"It helps you find the next useful conversation."]],
x:["Plan a 10-minute Mom Test conversation for a product idea of your own.","Pick a specific person who has the problem. Prepare three big questions about their current behaviour, e.g. 'How do you do this now?', 'What's the most annoying part?', 'What have you tried?'. Don't pitch. Take notes and end by asking for a small commitment and 'Who else should I talk to?'"]}]});
B({id:"psychology-of-money",title:"The Psychology of Money",author:"Morgan Housel",year:2020,topic:"Money",color:"#a07a1f",
big:"Doing well with money is less about what you know and more about how you behave: patience, humility and room for error.",
model:"Wealth = saving + time + staying in the game. Get rich with optimism, stay rich with paranoia: leave room for error so compounding can work for decades.",
points:["Everyone's money views come from their own experience: no one's crazy.","Luck and risk are siblings; judge decisions, not just outcomes.","Know what 'enough' is and stop risking what you need for what you don't.","Compounding needs time more than high returns.","Getting wealthy and staying wealthy need different skills.","Wealth is what you don't see: money not spent.","Your savings rate matters more than your income or returns.","Volatility is the fee for long-term returns, not a fine."],
s:[
{t:"Behaviour, luck and 'enough'",c:[
["No one's crazy","People make money decisions based on their own experiences. Someone who grew up through high inflation sees money differently from someone who grew up in a boom. Their choices can look crazy to others but make sense to them.","Think of one money belief you got from your upbringing."],
["Luck and risk","Bill Gates went to one of the few schools in the world with a computer in the late 1960s. His equally gifted friend Kent Evans died in a mountaineering accident before he could do the same. Housel's point: outcomes involve luck and risk, so judge decisions by how they were made, not just how they turned out.","Name a decision of yours that turned out well partly through luck."],
["Never enough","Some people with huge wealth risk everything for more. Housel tells of Rajat Gupta, a wealthy executive jailed for insider trading. The hardest money skill is getting the goalposts to stop moving and knowing what 'enough' is.","Write down what 'enough' looks like for your family."],
["Social comparison","There's always someone with more. If your benchmark is the people above you, you'll never feel you have enough and may take risks you'd regret. Choose your own scoreboard.","Who do you compare yourself with, and is it helping?"],
["Things you can't risk","Reputation, freedom, family and happiness are worth far more than extra money. Don't risk what you have and need for what you don't have and don't need.","List what you'd never risk for more money."]],
q:[["What's Housel's point about luck and risk?",["Successful people are just lucky","Judge decisions by how they were made, not only by outcomes","Risk should always be avoided","Luck evens out"],1,"Outcomes can hide good or bad decisions."],
["What does Housel call the hardest financial skill?",["Picking stocks","Getting the goalposts to stop moving","Budgeting","Negotiating salary"],1,"Knowing what 'enough' is."],
["Why does 'no one's crazy' about money?",["Everyone is rational","People act on their own experiences","Money is simple","Experts agree"],1,"Different lives produce different money views."]],
x:["Explain the idea of 'enough' and why it protects you.","Knowing what's enough stops the goalposts from moving. Without it, you keep chasing more and may risk things you already have and need, like reputation, family or freedom, for things you don't need. Enough lets you stop taking unnecessary risks."]},
{t:"Compounding and staying wealthy",c:[
["Confounding compounding","Warren Buffett is a great investor, but the main reason he's so rich is time: he started investing as a boy and kept going into his 90s. The vast majority of his wealth came after his mid-60s. Good returns sustained for a long time beat brilliant returns for a short time.","How many years could your investments compound for?"],
["Getting rich vs staying rich","Getting wealthy takes risk and optimism. Staying wealthy takes frugality and a healthy dose of paranoia: assume things will go wrong and make sure you survive them. Housel's key aim is never to be forced to sell or quit.","What could force you to sell investments at a bad time?"],
["Tails drive everything","A small number of events cause most results. Walt Disney's studio made many films that lost money, but Snow White's success paid for nearly everything. Most investments won't matter much; a few will matter hugely. You can be wrong half the time and still do well.","Accept that some of your choices will be duds."],
["Freedom is the real dividend","The greatest value of money is control over your time: being able to do what you want, when you want, with whom you want. Housel argues this matters more to happiness than a bigger house or car.","What would an extra hour of freedom a day be worth to you?"],
["The man in the car paradox","When you see someone in a flash car, you rarely think 'what a cool person'. You picture yourself in it. People seek respect through possessions, but others are mostly thinking about themselves. Kindness and humility earn more respect.","Think of a purchase you made partly to impress others."]],
q:[["What's the main driver of Buffett's wealth, according to Housel?",["Secret strategies","Time: investing for decades","Luck alone","High-risk bets"],1,"Compounding needs time above all."],
["What does staying wealthy require?",["Bigger risks","Frugality and some paranoia","Constant trading","Borrowing"],1,"Survive so compounding can keep working."],
["What does Housel call money's greatest value?",["Status","Control over your time","Possessions","Security from tax"],1,"Freedom to choose what you do with your days."]],
x:["Why does Housel say staying wealthy is different from getting wealthy?","Getting wealthy needs risk-taking and optimism. Staying wealthy needs humility, frugality and fear of losing it: avoiding ruin so you're never forced to sell or quit. Compounding only works if you survive long enough."]},
{t:"Saving, room for error and long-term thinking",c:[
["Wealth is what you don't see","Being rich (high current spending) and being wealthy (money not spent) are different. Wealth is the cars not bought and the upgrades not taken. Ronald Read, a petrol station attendant and janitor, quietly built a fortune of about $8m by saving and investing.","Notice one 'invisible' choice you could make this week."],
["Save without needing a reason","Your savings rate is something you control, unlike returns. You don't need a specific goal to save: saving for the unexpected gives you options and flexibility when life surprises you.","Set up an automatic saving, however small."],
["Reasonable over rational","A plan that's mathematically perfect but that you'll abandon in a panic is worse than a sensible one you'll stick to. Housel favours choices you can live with and sleep with.","Choose the simple plan you'll actually follow."],
["Room for error","History is full of surprises, so plan for things to go wrong. Keep a cash buffer, avoid too much debt and don't count on everything going to plan. A margin of safety lets you survive bad luck.","Check: could you cover several months of surprise costs?"],
["Volatility is a fee, not a fine","Market ups and downs are the price of long-term returns, like an entry fee to a theme park. Seeing it as a fine makes people sell at the worst times. Accepting it makes it easier to stay invested.","Decide in advance what you'll do (ideally nothing) in a crash."]],
q:[["What is 'wealth', in Housel's sense?",["What you spend","Money not spent","Your salary","Your house"],1,"Wealth is the assets you keep, often invisible."],
["Why plan with 'room for error'?",["To pay less tax","Because surprises happen and you need to survive them","To impress lenders","To trade more"],1,"A margin of safety keeps you in the game."],
["How does Housel describe market volatility?",["A fine for bad investing","A fee for long-term returns","Something to avoid entirely","A sign to sell"],1,"Pay the fee and stay invested."]],
x:["Which of Housel's ideas would most change your own money habits, and how?","A strong answer picks one idea (enough, room for error, saving without a reason, reasonable over rational, volatility as a fee) and links it to a concrete action, such as raising an automatic saving, building a cash buffer or writing a 'what I'll do in a crash' plan."]}]});

B({id:"thinking-fast-slow",title:"Thinking, Fast and Slow",author:"Daniel Kahneman",year:2011,topic:"Mindset",color:"#4a5a8c",
big:"Our fast, intuitive mind is usually right but makes predictable errors; knowing them helps you slow down when it matters.",
model:"System 1: fast, automatic, intuitive. System 2: slow, effortful, logical (and lazy). Know the biases System 1 produces: anchoring, availability, base-rate neglect, overconfidence, loss aversion and framing.",
points:["System 1 runs most of the time; System 2 checks it only when pushed.","WYSIATI: we build confident stories from whatever we see.","Anchors pull estimates towards any number we've just seen.","Availability: we judge likelihood by how easily examples come to mind.","We ignore base rates and are fooled by stories (the Linda problem).","The planning fallacy: use the outside view of similar projects.","Losses hurt about twice as much as equal gains feel good.","The remembering self judges experiences by their peak and their end."],
s:[
{t:"Two systems",c:[
["System 1 and System 2","System 1 is fast, automatic and intuitive: it reads faces, finishes '2 + 2 = ?' and drives on an empty road. System 2 is slow, effortful and deliberate: it works out 17 × 24 or fills in a tax form. We like to think System 2 is in charge, but System 1 does most of the work.","Notice one decision today you made on autopilot."],
["The bat and ball","A bat and ball cost £1.10. The bat costs £1 more than the ball. How much is the ball? Most people instantly say 10p. The answer is 5p. System 1 offers an easy answer and a lazy System 2 accepts it without checking.","Work it out slowly: why is 5p right?"],
["Cognitive ease","When something feels easy (familiar, clear, repeated), we tend to believe it and like it. That's why repeated claims feel true. Strain makes System 2 more alert and careful.","Be wary of claims that feel true mainly because you've heard them often."],
["What you see is all there is","System 1 builds the most coherent story it can from the information available and doesn't ask what's missing. Kahneman calls this WYSIATI. It explains why we jump to conclusions with total confidence.","Before deciding, ask: what information am I missing?"],
["The halo effect","If we like one thing about someone, we tend to like everything about them. A confident speaker seems competent; a good-looking candidate seems capable. First impressions spread to everything else.","Think of someone you judged too quickly, good or bad."]],
q:[["In the bat-and-ball puzzle, what does the ball cost?",["10p","5p","£1","£1.05"],1,"Bat £1.05 + ball 5p = £1.10, and the bat is £1 more."],
["What does WYSIATI stand for?",["What you see is all there is","Why your system ignores all thoughts","When you see it, act immediately","What you sense is always true"],0,"We build stories only from the information in front of us."],
["Which system is fast and intuitive?",["System 1","System 2","Both","Neither"],0,"System 2 is the slow, effortful one."]],
x:["Explain System 1 and System 2 with an example from your own life.","System 1 is fast and automatic, like recognising a friend's face or reacting to a car braking ahead. System 2 is slow and effortful, like comparing mortgage deals. Most of the time System 1 runs things and System 2 lazily accepts its answers unless something forces it to engage."]},
{t:"Heuristics and biases",c:[
["Anchoring","Any number you see before making an estimate pulls your answer towards it, even if it's random. That's why the first price in a negotiation is so powerful, and why 'was £200, now £99' works.","Before negotiating, set your own number first."],
["Availability","We judge how common something is by how easily examples come to mind. After a plane crash is in the news, people overestimate the risk of flying. Vivid and recent beats accurate.","Ask whether a risk feels big because it's common or because it's memorable."],
["The Linda problem","Linda is described as bright, outspoken and concerned with social justice. Is she more likely to be a bank teller, or a bank teller who is active in the feminist movement? Most people choose the second, but it can't be more likely than the first. A good story beats logic.","Watch for detailed stories that feel more likely than they are."],
["Ignoring base rates","We focus on a vivid description and ignore how common something is overall. If a description sounds like a librarian, we forget that there are far more salespeople than librarians. Start with the base rate, then adjust.","For your next judgement, ask: how common is this in general?"],
["Regression to the mean","Kahneman tells of flight instructors who believed shouting after a bad landing worked, because the next landing was usually better. But exceptional performances, good or bad, are usually followed by more average ones anyway. Don't read too much into a single great or terrible result.","Think of a time you over-reacted to one unusual result."]],
q:[["What is anchoring?",["Sticking to a decision","Being pulled towards a number you saw earlier","Refusing to change","A savings habit"],1,"Even random numbers bias estimates."],
["Why do people overestimate flight risk after a crash?",["Anchoring","Availability","Regression","Framing"],1,"Vivid examples come easily to mind."],
["What does regression to the mean predict after an exceptionally bad result?",["Another bad result","A more average result","An exceptional good result","No change"],1,"Extremes tend to be followed by more average results."]],
x:["Pick one bias from this session and explain how it could trip you up.","For example, anchoring: when buying a car, the dealer's first price pulls my sense of a fair deal towards it, even if it's inflated. To guard against it, I'd research and set my own target price before seeing theirs."]},
{t:"Overconfidence, choices and the two selves",c:[
["The planning fallacy","We expect projects to take less time and money than they do because we picture the best case. Kahneman's own team once estimated two years to write a curriculum; it took about eight. The fix is the 'outside view': check how long similar projects actually took.","For your next project, look up how long similar ones really took."],
["The premortem","Before committing to a plan, imagine it's a year later and the plan has failed. Spend five minutes writing why. Psychologist Gary Klein's technique legitimises doubts and surfaces risks that groupthink hides.","Run a 5-minute premortem on a current plan."],
["Loss aversion","Losing £100 feels roughly twice as bad as winning £100 feels good. That makes us cling to losing investments, avoid sensible risks and value things more once we own them (the endowment effect).","Ask: am I avoiding a loss or chasing a real gain?"],
["Framing","The same facts presented differently lead to different choices. A treatment with a '90% survival rate' sounds far better than one with a '10% death rate', though they're identical. Always try restating options the other way round.","Reframe one decision you're weighing as a gain and as a loss."],
["Two selves","The experiencing self lives the moments; the remembering self keeps score. The remembering self judges an experience mostly by its peak and how it ended (the peak-end rule) and largely ignores how long it lasted. A great ending can redeem a so-so holiday.","Plan a strong ending for your next family day out."]],
q:[["What's the remedy for the planning fallacy?",["Plan in more detail","Take the outside view of similar projects","Work faster","Ignore deadlines"],1,"Reference similar real projects, not your best case."],
["Roughly how much worse do losses feel than equal gains?",["About the same","About twice as bad","Ten times as bad","Half as bad"],1,"Loss aversion is roughly 2 to 1."],
["What does the peak-end rule say?",["We remember experiences by their average","We remember the peak moment and the end","We remember the start best","We remember everything equally"],1,"Duration matters surprisingly little."]],
x:["How could you use the premortem or outside view on a real decision?","Example: before booking a home project, I'd look up how long similar jobs took others (outside view) and add a buffer. Then I'd imagine it's gone wrong and list why (premortem): materials late, hidden damage, weather. That gives me a more realistic plan and backups."]}]});

B({id:"mindset",title:"Mindset",author:"Carol Dweck",year:2006,topic:"Mindset",color:"#b0466f",
big:"Believing your abilities can grow changes how you handle challenge, effort, setbacks and criticism.",
model:"Fixed mindset: ability is set, so avoid challenge, see effort as weakness, hide failure. Growth mindset: ability grows, so seek challenge, value effort, learn from failure and criticism.",
points:["A fixed mindset believes ability is set; a growth mindset believes it can be developed.","Mindset shapes how we respond to challenge, effort, setbacks and criticism.","Praising intelligence can make people avoid challenges; praising process builds resilience.","In a growth mindset, failure is information.","Effort, good strategies and help from others all drive growth.","Leaders and teams with a growth mindset learn and adapt better.","Everyone has fixed-mindset triggers; the aim is to spot them.","'Not yet' turns a verdict into a stage of learning."],
s:[
{t:"Two mindsets",c:[
["Fixed vs growth","With a fixed mindset, you believe your intelligence and talents are fixed traits, so every task is a test of how good you are. With a growth mindset, you believe abilities can be developed through effort, good strategies and help from others. It isn't about believing anyone can do anything; it's about believing you can improve.","Name one ability you've assumed is fixed in you."],
["Challenges","Fixed: avoid challenges in case you look less able. Growth: seek challenges because that's where learning happens. Dweck found children with a growth mindset chose harder puzzles when given the choice.","Pick one challenge you've been avoiding."],
["Effort","Fixed: if you have to try hard, you must not be talented, so effort feels like weakness. Growth: effort is what makes you talented. Mastery takes work for everyone.","Recall something that only got easy after real effort."],
["Setbacks","Fixed: failure says something permanent about you ('I'm a failure'). Growth: failure is information about what to do differently ('that approach failed').","Reword one past failure as information."],
["Criticism and others' success","Fixed: criticism feels like an attack, and others' success feels like a threat. Growth: criticism is useful feedback, and others' success is a source of lessons and inspiration.","Ask someone for honest feedback on something you're working on."]],
q:[["What does a growth mindset believe?",["Anyone can do anything","Abilities can be developed through effort, strategy and help","Talent doesn't exist","Effort is all that matters"],1,"It's about improvement, not limitless potential."],
["How does a fixed mindset view effort?",["As the path to mastery","As a sign you lack talent","As fun","As unnecessary"],1,"Growth mindset sees effort as what builds ability."],
["How does a growth mindset view criticism?",["As an attack","As useful feedback","As irrelevant","As jealousy"],1,"It's information for improving."]],
x:["Describe the difference between the two mindsets in your own words.","A fixed mindset thinks ability is set, so it avoids challenges, sees effort as a weakness, treats failure as a verdict and takes criticism personally. A growth mindset thinks ability can grow, so it seeks challenges, values effort, learns from failure and uses criticism as feedback."]},
{t:"Praise, sport and business",c:[
["The praise study","Dweck's team gave children puzzles. Some were praised for being clever, others for working hard. The 'clever' group then avoided harder puzzles, enjoyed them less after struggling, and more of them exaggerated their scores. The 'effort' group sought challenge and improved.","Notice how you praise others this week."],
["Praise the process","Praise effort, strategies, focus and persistence: 'You stuck with that and tried a new approach.' Praising talent ('You're a natural') can teach people to avoid anything that might show they're not.","Rephrase one compliment to praise the process."],
["Talent isn't enough","In sport, Dweck argues 'naturals' often plateau when things get hard because they never learned to work, while those who see ability as built keep improving. Character, the ability to dig in when things go wrong, matters.","Think of a 'natural' you know who stopped improving."],
["Growth-mindset leaders","Dweck contrasts leaders who need to look superior and surround themselves with yes-men with those who keep learning, admit mistakes and develop their people. Companies that worship innate talent can discourage honesty and learning.","Ask yourself: do I create space for people to admit mistakes?"],
["Relationships","In a fixed mindset, a problem in a relationship can feel like proof of a flaw in you, your partner or the relationship. In a growth mindset, problems are things to work through together.","Think of one disagreement as something to solve together."]],
q:[["In Dweck's praise study, what happened to children praised for being clever?",["They chose harder puzzles","They avoided harder puzzles","They improved most","Nothing changed"],1,"Talent praise made them protect their 'clever' label."],
["Which is process praise?",["'You're so smart'","'You're a natural'","'You kept trying different strategies'","'You're the best'"],2,"It praises what the person did."],
["What does a growth-mindset leader do?",["Hides mistakes","Surrounds themselves with yes-men","Admits mistakes and develops people","Relies on talent"],2,"They keep learning and help others learn."]],
x:["Why can praising intelligence backfire?","It teaches people that their worth depends on looking clever, so they avoid challenges where they might struggle or fail, give up sooner and may hide mistakes. Praising effort and strategy teaches that struggle is part of improving."]},
{t:"Changing your mindset",c:[
["Mindsets are mixed","No one has a pure growth mindset. Everyone has a mix, and certain situations trigger a fixed mindset: criticism, a big setback, or meeting someone better than you. The aim is to notice, not to be perfect.","List two situations that trigger your fixed mindset."],
["Name your fixed-mindset persona","Dweck suggests giving your fixed-mindset voice a name. When it pipes up ('You'll look stupid'), you can recognise it, talk back and choose a growth response.","Give your fixed-mindset voice a name."],
["The power of 'yet'","Adding 'yet' changes a verdict into a stage: 'I can't do this yet.' It keeps the door open to improving.","Add 'yet' to one thing you've written off."],
["Make a concrete plan","Good intentions aren't enough. Plan when, where and how you'll take on a challenge, and what you'll do when it gets hard. Growth needs action, not just belief.","Write: 'When it gets hard, I will…'"],
["False growth mindset","Dweck warns that a growth mindset isn't just praising effort that isn't working, or saying 'anyone can do anything'. It's about learning: trying new strategies and seeking help when you're stuck.","When stuck, ask: what strategy could I try next?"]],
q:[["Which is true about mindsets?",["People are purely one or the other","Everyone has a mix and certain triggers","Mindsets can't change","Only children have them"],1,"The goal is to notice your triggers."],
["Why name your fixed-mindset persona?",["For fun","To recognise its voice and respond differently","To blame it","To ignore feelings"],1,"Naming it makes it easier to spot and answer."],
["What does 'false growth mindset' get wrong?",["Valuing learning","Praising effort that isn't working instead of changing strategy","Seeking help","Taking on challenges"],1,"Growth means learning and trying new strategies."]],
x:["Describe one fixed-mindset trigger of yours and how you'll respond next time.","A good answer names a real trigger (e.g. a colleague doing something better, harsh feedback, a failed attempt), the fixed-mindset thought it brings, and a specific growth response: what you'll tell yourself, a new strategy to try or someone to ask for help."]}]});

B({id:"co-intelligence",title:"Co-Intelligence",author:"Ethan Mollick",year:2024,topic:"AI",color:"#1f7a80",
big:"AI is a strange, powerful co-worker: use it widely, stay in charge, give it a role, and expect it to keep improving.",
model:"Four rules: Always invite AI to the table. Be the human in the loop. Treat AI like a person (but tell it what kind). Assume this is the worst AI you will ever use.",
points:["Large language models predict likely next words; they're powerful but alien.","AI's abilities form a 'jagged frontier': strong on some tasks, weak on similar-looking ones.","In a study with consultants, AI users did better on tasks inside the frontier and worse outside it.","Work as a centaur (split tasks) or a cyborg (blend constantly).","Stay the human in the loop: AI can be confidently wrong.","Giving AI context and a persona improves results.","AI can be a creative partner, co-worker, tutor and coach.","Plan for several futures, from slow progress to rapid change."],
s:[
{t:"Understanding the alien mind",c:[
["A new kind of mind","Large language models are trained on vast amounts of text to predict what comes next. That simple goal produces surprisingly human-like abilities, but they don't think like us. Mollick calls AI an 'alien mind' that we must learn to work with.","Note one thing AI surprised you by doing well, and one it did badly."],
["Hallucinations","Because AI predicts plausible text, it can produce confident, convincing answers that are false, such as made-up facts or references. It's not lying; it doesn't know what it doesn't know.","Always check facts, figures and sources AI gives you."],
["The jagged frontier","AI's abilities are uneven. It may write a brilliant essay and then fail a simple counting puzzle. The line between what it can and can't do is jagged and invisible, so you only find it by trying.","Keep a note of tasks where AI helps you and where it doesn't."],
["The consultant study","Mollick and colleagues studied consultants at Boston Consulting Group. On tasks inside the frontier, those using AI finished more tasks, faster and to a higher quality. On a task outside it, AI users were more likely to get the answer wrong, because they trusted it too much.","Ask: is this task inside or outside AI's frontier?"],
["Centaurs and cyborgs","Centaurs divide the work: they hand some tasks to AI and do others themselves. Cyborgs blend constantly, working with AI on every step. Both can work; the key is staying engaged.","Which style do you use with AI now?"]],
q:[["What does the 'jagged frontier' describe?",["AI's uneven abilities across similar tasks","The border of AI regulation","The speed of AI","AI hardware limits"],0,"Strong at some tasks, surprisingly weak at others."],
["In the consultant study, what happened on the task outside the frontier?",["AI users did better","AI users were more likely to be wrong","No difference","AI refused"],1,"Over-trust hurt performance outside the frontier."],
["What is an AI 'hallucination'?",["A creative image","A confident but false answer","A system crash","A security breach"],1,"Plausible-sounding but wrong output."]],
x:["Explain the 'jagged frontier' and why it matters at work.","AI is excellent at some tasks and poor at others that look similar, and you can't see the boundary in advance. It matters because trusting AI on a task outside the frontier can make your work worse, so you need to test it and stay in charge of checking."]},
{t:"The four rules",c:[
["Rule 1: always invite AI to the table","Try AI on everything you legally and ethically can. Only by using it widely do you learn where it helps and where it doesn't, and you become the expert on AI in your own work.","Use AI on one task you'd never thought to try."],
["Rule 2: be the human in the loop","Stay in charge. Check AI's work, apply your judgement and own the result. It keeps your skills sharp and catches errors.","Review one AI output critically before using it."],
["Rule 3: treat AI like a person (but tell it what kind)","AI works better when you give it a role and context: 'You're a tough editor. Critique this for a busy executive.' Just remember it isn't actually a person.","Start your next prompt with a role, a goal and context."],
["Rule 4: assume this is the worst AI you'll use","AI is improving fast. Whatever frustrates you today may work in months. Plan and learn with that in mind.","Retry a task AI failed at a few months ago."],
["Prompting basics","Mollick's tips: give context and examples, explain the goal, ask it to think step by step, and iterate as in a conversation rather than expecting the perfect answer first time.","Ask AI to improve its own answer after the first try."]],
q:[["What does 'invite AI to the table' mean?",["Use AI only for coding","Try AI on everything you legally and ethically can","Let AI make decisions","Ban AI at work"],1,"Wide use reveals where it helps."],
["Why give AI a persona?",["It's required","It focuses the output and improves results","It's polite","It makes AI conscious"],1,"Role plus context gives better answers."],
["What should you assume about today's AI?",["It's as good as it will get","It's the worst you'll ever use","It will disappear","It's only for experts"],1,"The tools keep improving."]],
x:["Which of the four rules would change how you use AI the most?","A strong answer picks a rule and a concrete habit, e.g. 'be the human in the loop: I'll check every figure and source AI gives me before using it', or 'give it a persona: I'll start prompts with a role, goal and context'."]},
{t:"AI as co-worker, tutor and coach",c:[
["AI as a creative partner","AI is very good at producing lots of ideas quickly. Ask it for 30 options, then use your judgement to pick and combine. Quantity of ideas tends to raise quality.","Ask AI for 20 ideas on a current problem."],
["AI as a co-worker","Mollick sorts tasks into 'just me' tasks (keep human, for value or ethics), delegated tasks (AI drafts, you check) and automated tasks (AI handles end to end, rarely, with care). It's tasks, not whole jobs, that change first.","Sort your weekly tasks into these three groups."],
["AI as a tutor","AI can explain ideas at your level, quiz you and adapt. It can't replace expertise you need to judge its answers, so learning the basics yourself still matters.","Ask AI to quiz you on something you're learning."],
["AI as a coach","AI can give feedback, role-play difficult conversations and challenge your thinking. Ask it to argue against your plan to find weak spots.","Ask AI to play devil's advocate on a decision."],
["Four possible futures","Mollick sketches scenarios: AI stops improving much; it improves slowly; it improves exponentially; or it becomes something far more powerful. He argues we should plan for change rather than assume any single future.","Think: what would change in your work if AI doubled in ability?"]],
q:[["What's a 'just me' task?",["One AI does entirely","One you keep human for value or ethics","One you delegate to colleagues","One you avoid"],1,"Some tasks should stay human."],
["How can AI help you think about a decision?",["By deciding for you","By arguing against your plan to expose weak spots","By hiding risks","By agreeing"],1,"Use it as a challenging coach."],
["What's Mollick's view on AI's future?",["Certain to stall","Certain to explode","Plan for several possible futures","Irrelevant"],2,"Prepare for change rather than one prediction."]],
x:["Sort three tasks from your week into 'just me', delegated and automated, and explain why.","Example: 'just me' = giving feedback to my team (needs trust and judgement); delegated = drafting a project update (AI drafts, I check facts); automated = summarising meeting notes into actions (low risk, easy to check). The reasoning should weigh value, risk and how easily mistakes would be spotted."]}]});
B({id:"essentialism",title:"Essentialism",author:"Greg McKeown",year:2014,topic:"Productivity",color:"#8a5a2b",
big:"Do less, but better: deliberately choose the vital few things and say no to the rest.",
model:"Explore (discern the vital few) → Eliminate (cut the trivial many) → Execute (make doing the vital few effortless).",
points:["Essentialism is the disciplined pursuit of less.","Three truths: we can choose; almost everything is noise; trade-offs are real.","Create space to think, play and sleep: they sharpen judgement.","Use extreme criteria: if it isn't a clear yes, it's a no.","Set an essential intent: concrete and inspiring.","Say no gracefully; separate the decision from the relationship.","Build buffers and remove obstacles before they slow you down.","Small, visible wins and routines make the essential effortless."],
s:[
{t:"The essentialist mindset and explore",c:[
["Less, but better","McKeown borrows designer Dieter Rams's phrase 'less, but better'. A non-essentialist tries to do everything and makes a millimetre of progress in a million directions. An essentialist puts their energy into the few things that matter most.","List your current commitments. Which few really matter?"],
["Three core truths","You can choose how to spend your time and energy. Almost everything is noise; very little is truly essential. And trade-offs are real: you can't have it all, so the question is which problem you want.","Name one trade-off you've been avoiding."],
["If you don't prioritise your life, someone else will","When we don't choose deliberately, other people's priorities fill our time. Saying yes to everything means saying no to what matters by default.","Notice whose priorities filled your week."],
["Escape: space to think","Essentialists build time into their week just to think. Some leaders block off hours in their diary with no meetings. Without space, we can't tell what's essential.","Block 30 minutes this week with no agenda."],
["Look, play, sleep","Look for what really matters, like a journalist finding the lede. Play fuels creativity. And sleep is not a luxury: McKeown says protecting the asset (you) is what makes good judgement possible.","Pick one of look, play or sleep to protect this week."]],
q:[["What phrase sums up essentialism?",["Work hard, play hard","Less, but better","More is more","Fail fast"],1,"Borrowed from designer Dieter Rams."],
["Which is one of McKeown's three core truths?",["Trade-offs are real","Busy means important","Yes is always polite","Everything matters equally"],0,"You can't have it all, so choose."],
["Why does McKeown value sleep and play?",["They're rewards","They protect the judgement you need to choose well","They're unproductive","Only for weekends"],1,"Protect the asset: you."]],
x:["Explain the three core truths of essentialism in your own words.","1) We have choices about where our time and energy go. 2) Most things are noise; only a few really matter. 3) Trade-offs are unavoidable, so instead of asking how to do it all, ask which few things to go big on."]},
{t:"Eliminate",c:[
["Select: extreme criteria","Score each option out of 100. If it doesn't hit about 90, treat it as a zero. Applying tough criteria frees you from 'quite good' options that crowd out great ones.","Run one decision through the 90% test."],
["Clarify: essential intent","Teams and people drift without clear purpose. An essential intent is concrete and inspiring at once, so it rules things in and out. Ask: 'If we could be truly excellent at only one thing, what would it be?'","Write an essential intent for one project."],
["Dare: the graceful no","Separate the decision from the relationship. Useful ways to say no include 'Let me check my calendar and get back to you', 'I can't do it, but I'd be happy to…', or suggesting someone else who can help. A clear no often earns more respect than a reluctant yes.","Practise one graceful no this week."],
["Uncommit: cut your losses","We stick with failing commitments because we've already invested (sunk cost), we already own them (endowment effect) or we fear admitting a mistake. Ask: 'If I weren't already doing this, how much would I invest to start it now?'","Apply that question to one ongoing commitment."],
["Edit and limit","Like a good editor, cut what doesn't add value, and do more by doing less. Set boundaries in advance so you don't have to decide each time.","Cut one recurring meeting or task."]],
q:[["What's the 90% rule?",["Work at 90% effort","If an option scores under about 90/100, treat it as a no","Say yes 90% of the time","Spend 90% on email"],1,"It filters out 'quite good' options."],
["Which question helps you uncommit?",["'What will people think?'","'If I weren't doing this, how much would I invest to start it now?'","'How much have I spent?'","'Who started this?'"],1,"It cuts through sunk costs."],
["What makes a good essential intent?",["Vague and inspiring","Concrete and inspiring","Long and detailed","Secret"],1,"Concrete enough to guide choices."]],
x:["Write a graceful 'no' to a request you might get, using McKeown's ideas.","Example: 'Thanks for thinking of me. I can't take this on at the moment because I'm focused on [priority], but [name] would be great, or I could review the final draft next week.' It separates the decision from the relationship and offers an alternative."]},
{t:"Execute",c:[
["Buffer","Plans go wrong, so build in buffers. McKeown uses the biblical story of Joseph advising Egypt to store grain during seven good years to survive seven lean ones. Add about 50% to time estimates for important work.","Add a buffer to your next deadline."],
["Subtract: remove obstacles","Instead of pushing harder, find the biggest constraint slowing you down and remove it. Fixing the bottleneck speeds up everything.","Name the single biggest obstacle on your top project."],
["Progress: small wins","Celebrate and build on small, visible wins. Start small and get big. Visible progress is motivating, which keeps the essential work moving.","Set a 'minimum viable progress' goal for today."],
["Flow: routine","Make the essential the default with routines, so it doesn't need willpower. Change one habit at a time and attach it to a cue.","Build one routine around your most important task."],
["Focus and be","Ask 'What's important now?' and give it your full attention. McKeown ends by arguing essentialism isn't a one-off; it's a way of living that helps you live a life that really matters.","Ask 'What's important now?' three times today."]],
q:[["Why build a buffer?",["To slow down","Because plans go wrong and you need room for the unexpected","To impress others","To avoid work"],1,"Joseph stored grain for the lean years."],
["What does 'subtract' mean in execution?",["Do fewer hours","Remove the biggest obstacle","Spend less","Cut staff"],1,"Fix the bottleneck."],
["Which question keeps you focused on the present?",["'What's next?'","'What's important now?'","'Who's watching?'","'What did I miss?'"],1,"Full attention on what matters right now."]],
x:["Apply Explore, Eliminate and Execute to one area of your life.","A strong answer names an area (e.g. evenings), explores what matters (family time, training), eliminates something specific (scrolling, an unneeded commitment) and sets up execution (a routine, a buffer, removing an obstacle like leaving the phone downstairs)."]}]});

B({id:"getting-things-done",title:"Getting Things Done",author:"David Allen",year:2001,topic:"Productivity",color:"#5a7a2b",
big:"Get everything out of your head into a trusted system, decide the next action for each item, and review weekly.",
model:"Five steps: Capture → Clarify → Organise → Reflect → Engage. Key question for every item: 'What's the next action?'",
points:["Your mind is for having ideas, not holding them.","Open loops (unfinished commitments) drain attention.","Capture everything into a few trusted inboxes.","Clarify: is it actionable? If not, bin, file or 'someday/maybe'.","If it takes under two minutes, do it now; otherwise delegate or defer.","Any outcome needing more than one step is a project with a next action.","Keep the calendar for time-specific things only.","The weekly review keeps the system trusted."],
s:[
{t:"Capture and clarify",c:[
["Mind like water","Allen describes a calm, ready state in which you can respond to whatever comes, like still water reacting to a pebble. It's only possible when your head isn't cluttered with unfinished commitments.","Rate how cluttered your mind feels today, 1-10."],
["Open loops","Anything you've committed to that isn't done, from 'fix the gate' to 'plan the party', is an open loop. Your brain keeps nagging about it, often at the wrong time. Getting it out of your head and into a system closes the nagging.","Write down five open loops right now."],
["Capture everything","Collect every commitment, idea and task into a small number of inboxes: a notebook, a phone app, an email inbox, a physical tray. The key is capturing everything, so your mind can trust nothing is lost.","Choose your one main capture tool."],
["Clarify: what is it?","Process each item: what is it, and is it actionable? If not, bin it, file it as reference, or put it on a 'someday/maybe' list. If it is, decide the very next physical action.","Clarify five items in your inbox."],
["The next action","'Sort the car' is vague. 'Call the garage to book an MOT' is a next action: a visible, physical step. Vague tasks get procrastinated; next actions get done.","Turn one vague task into a next action."]],
q:[["What does Allen mean by an 'open loop'?",["An unfinished commitment","A computer bug","A free afternoon","A meeting"],0,"Open loops nag at your attention."],
["What's the first question when clarifying an item?",["How long will it take?","Is it actionable?","Who sent it?","Is it urgent?"],1,"Then decide what to do with it."],
["Which is a next action?",["'Sort the car'","'Car stuff'","'Call the garage to book an MOT'","'Think about car'"],2,"A specific, visible physical step."]],
x:["Explain why capturing everything reduces stress.","When commitments live only in your head, your mind keeps reminding you of them, often when you can't act. Getting them all into a trusted system means your mind can let go and focus, because nothing will be forgotten."]},
{t:"Organise",c:[
["The two-minute rule","If an action takes less than two minutes, do it now. It would take longer to store and track it than to finish it.","Do every two-minute task in your inbox today."],
["Delegate or defer","If it takes longer, decide: delegate it (and track it on a 'waiting for' list) or defer it (put it on your next-actions list or calendar).","Start a 'waiting for' list."],
["Projects","Allen defines a project as any outcome that needs more than one action and can be done within a year. 'Plan Joshua's party' is a project. Keep a projects list, and make sure each one has a next action.","List your current projects."],
["Contexts","Organise next actions by context: calls, computer, errands, at home, at the office. Then, wherever you are, you can see what you can actually do there.","Make an 'Errands' list for your next trip out."],
["The calendar is sacred","Put only time-specific things on your calendar: appointments, deadlines, things that must happen on a particular day. Don't fill it with 'to-dos' you hope to get to, or you'll stop trusting it.","Remove one 'hope to do' item from your calendar."]],
q:[["What should you do with a task that takes under two minutes?",["Schedule it","Delegate it","Do it now","Add it to a project"],2,"Tracking it would take longer."],
["How does GTD define a project?",["Any big task","Any outcome needing more than one action","Only work tasks","Tasks with a deadline"],1,"Most people have many more projects than they think."],
["What belongs on the calendar?",["Everything","Only time-specific actions and information","Ideas","Waiting-for items"],1,"Keep it trusted."]],
x:["Walk through what you'd do with this item: 'Email from school about a trip form'.","Clarify: actionable? Yes. Next action: fill in and return the form. Under two minutes? If so, do it now. If not, put it on next actions (computer context) and add the deadline to the calendar if there is one. Archive the email as reference."]},
{t:"Reflect and engage",c:[
["The weekly review","Once a week, get clear (empty all inboxes, capture loose ends), get current (update projects, next actions, waiting-for and calendar) and get creative (review someday/maybe and new ideas). It's what keeps the whole system trustworthy.","Book a 30-minute weekly review."],
["Horizons of focus","Allen describes levels of perspective, from the runway (current actions) up through projects, areas of responsibility, goals, vision and purpose. The weekly review mostly covers the lower levels; review the higher ones less often.","Note your main areas of responsibility (work, family, health…)."],
["Natural planning","For projects, plan the way your mind naturally works: purpose and principles (why), outcome vision (what success looks like), brainstorm, organise, then next actions.","Use the five steps on one project."],
["Choosing what to do","In the moment, choose by context (what can I do here?), time available, energy available, then priority. Matching tasks to your energy avoids wasted time.","Match your next task to your current energy."],
["Someday/maybe","Ideas you're not committing to now, like a trip or a course, go on a someday/maybe list, reviewed weekly. They're out of your head but not lost.","Start a someday/maybe list with three items."]],
q:[["What are the three parts of the weekly review?",["Plan, do, check","Get clear, get current, get creative","Sort, delete, file","Morning, noon, night"],1,"It keeps the system up to date and trusted."],
["Which comes first in natural planning?",["Next actions","Brainstorm","Purpose and principles","Organise"],2,"Start with why."],
["What four things guide what to do in the moment?",["Context, time, energy, priority","Urgency, importance, cost, value","Boss, deadline, mood, weather","Size, colour, order, list"],0,"Match tasks to where you are and how you feel."]],
x:["Describe how you'd run your own weekly review.","Get clear: empty email, notes and paper into the inbox and process them. Get current: update the projects list (each has a next action), next actions, waiting-for and calendar for the next two weeks. Get creative: look at someday/maybe and capture new ideas. About 30-60 minutes, same time each week."]}]});

B({id:"e-myth",title:"The E-Myth Revisited",author:"Michael E. Gerber",year:1995,topic:"Business",color:"#6b3f8f",
big:"Most small businesses are started by technicians; to succeed, work on the business, building systems that work without you.",
model:"Three personalities: Entrepreneur (vision), Manager (order), Technician (doing). Build a 'franchise prototype' through Innovation, Quantification and Orchestration.",
points:["The E-Myth: people who know the technical work assume they can run the business.","Every owner is part entrepreneur, manager and technician.","Businesses pass through infancy, adolescence and maturity.","Work on the business, not just in it.","Build it as if it will be franchised: systems, not stars.","Document everything so ordinary people can deliver great results.","Innovate, quantify and orchestrate: improve, measure, standardise.","Start with your primary aim: what you want your life to look like."],
s:[
{t:"The E-Myth and the three personalities",c:[
["The fatal assumption","Gerber's E-Myth (entrepreneurial myth) is that most businesses are started by entrepreneurs. In fact they're usually started by technicians who have an 'entrepreneurial seizure' and assume that knowing the technical work means knowing how to run a business that does it.","Ask: what parts of running a business do I not know yet?"],
["Sarah's pie shop","Gerber tells of Sarah, who loved baking and opened a pie shop. Soon she was working all hours, hating the thing she loved, because the business depended entirely on her doing everything.","Imagine your idea in year two: who does all the work?"],
["The entrepreneur","The visionary who lives in the future, sees opportunities and asks 'What if?'. Without the entrepreneur, a business has no direction.","Write one sentence of vision for your idea."],
["The manager","The planner who lives in the past, wants order and asks 'How?'. Without the manager, there's chaos.","What would the 'manager' in you organise first?"],
["The technician","The doer who lives in the present and likes to work, not think. Gerber says most small business owners are about 10% entrepreneur, 20% manager and 70% technician, and the technician resents both of the others.","Which of the three dominates you?"]],
q:[["What is the E-Myth's 'fatal assumption'?",["Businesses need investors","Knowing the technical work means knowing how to run the business","Marketing is everything","Small is better"],1,"Technical skill and business skill differ."],
["Which personality asks 'What if?'",["Entrepreneur","Manager","Technician","Accountant"],0,"The visionary looks to the future."],
["What went wrong for Sarah's pie shop?",["Bad pies","The business depended entirely on her doing everything","No customers","High rent"],1,"She was trapped working in it."]],
x:["Describe the three personalities and which one is strongest in you.","Entrepreneur: vision, future, 'what if'. Manager: order, planning, systems, 'how'. Technician: hands-on doing, present. A good answer is honest about which dominates (often the technician for practical people) and what that means: e.g. great at the work, needs to make time for vision and systems."]},
{t:"Stages and working on the business",c:[
["Infancy","At first, the owner is the business. Everything depends on you, and that's fine for a while, until you can't keep up.","How long could you keep up doing everything yourself?"],
["Adolescence","The owner gets help, often by handing tasks to someone and walking away ('management by abdication'). When it goes wrong, the owner takes everything back and is soon overwhelmed again.","Think of a time you delegated and walked away. What happened?"],
["Maturity","Mature businesses are built from the start with a clear vision of how they'll work. Gerber says they begin with an entrepreneurial perspective, not a technical one.","Picture your business working well without you for a week."],
["Work on it, not in it","If you're always working in the business, nobody is designing it. Your job as owner is to build a business that works, even without you.","Block one hour a week to work on, not in, your project."],
["The business is not your life","Gerber argues a business should serve your life, not consume it. Start by asking what you want your life to look like, then design the business to deliver it.","Write how many hours a week you'd want the business to need from you."]],
q:[["What is 'management by abdication'?",["Delegating with clear systems","Handing off tasks and walking away","Firing staff","Doing everything yourself"],1,"It usually ends with the owner taking everything back."],
["In infancy, what is the business?",["A system","The owner","A franchise","A brand"],1,"Everything depends on the owner."],
["What should your business serve, according to Gerber?",["Your customers only","Your life","Your investors","Your ego"],1,"Design the business around the life you want."]],
x:["Explain what 'work on your business, not in it' means.","Working in it means doing the day-to-day technical work. Working on it means designing how the business runs: its systems, roles, processes and strategy, so it can deliver results consistently without depending on the owner doing everything."]},
{t:"The franchise prototype",c:[
["The turnkey revolution","Gerber points to Ray Kroc and McDonald's: the genius wasn't the burger but a system so well designed that ordinary people could deliver the same result anywhere. The business itself became the product.","What would a 'turnkey' version of your idea look like?"],
["Rules of the prototype","Gerber's prototype provides consistent value, can be run by people with the lowest possible level of skill, is in impeccable order, is documented in operations manuals, delivers a predictable experience, and uses consistent colours, dress and facilities.","Which rule would be hardest for your idea?"],
["Innovation, quantification, orchestration","Innovate: try better ways of doing things. Quantify: measure the impact. Orchestrate: once it works, make it the standard way every time.","Pick one process to measure this week."],
["Systems, not stars","Gerber argues great businesses get extraordinary results from ordinary people through great systems, rather than relying on rare superstars.","Write the steps of one task so someone else could do it."],
["The development programme","Gerber's seven steps: your primary aim (life goal), strategic objective (what the business must achieve), organisational strategy (roles on an org chart, even if you fill them all), management, people, marketing and systems strategies.","Draw a simple org chart of roles for your idea, even if you fill all of them."]],
q:[["What was Ray Kroc's real innovation, in Gerber's view?",["The burger recipe","A system ordinary people could run to the same standard","Low prices","Advertising"],1,"The business itself became the product."],
["What does 'orchestration' mean?",["Hiring musicians","Making the proven way the standard every time","Measuring results","Brainstorming"],1,"Innovate, quantify, then orchestrate."],
["What's the first step of Gerber's development programme?",["Marketing strategy","Primary aim","Systems strategy","Hiring"],1,"Start with what you want your life to be."]],
x:["How would you apply the franchise prototype to a business idea of your own?","Imagine it will be copied many times. Write down each key process step by step so someone with less skill could follow it, define the roles on an org chart (even if you fill them all), decide what to measure, and set standards for the customer experience."]}]});

B({id:"never-split",title:"Never Split the Difference",author:"Chris Voss",year:2016,topic:"Business",color:"#9b2f2f",
big:"Negotiation is emotional, not just rational: listen, label feelings and ask calibrated questions to get the other side to solve your problem.",
model:"Tactical empathy: Mirror → Label → Accusation audit → Aim for 'That's right' → Calibrated 'How/What' questions → Ackerman bargaining.",
points:["People decide emotionally, then justify it.","Use a calm, slow 'late-night radio' voice to calm things down.","Mirror: repeat the last few words to keep them talking.","Label emotions: 'It seems like…'","Run an accusation audit: name the negatives first.","Aim for 'That's right', not 'You're right'.","Ask calibrated how/what questions to make them solve the problem.","Bargain with the Ackerman model and look for hidden 'black swans'."],
s:[
{t:"Listening as a weapon",c:[
["Emotion beats logic","Voss, a former FBI lead international kidnapping negotiator, argues that the classic 'rational' approach to negotiation misses how people really decide. We're driven by emotion and then justify our choices. Understanding feelings is the key.","In your next tricky chat, listen for feelings, not just facts."],
["The late-night radio voice","A slow, calm, downward-inflecting voice signals control and calms people. Voss uses it to say things like 'That's not going to work.' Most of the time, he suggests a warm, positive, playful tone instead.","Practise saying no slowly and calmly."],
["Mirroring","Repeat the last one to three words someone said, as a question: 'The delivery's late?' It shows you're listening and gets them to say more. It feels odd but works.","Try one mirror in a conversation today."],
["Silence","After a mirror or a label, stay quiet. People tend to fill silence with more information. Resist the urge to jump in.","Count to four after your next question."],
["Your goal: discovery","Go into a negotiation to learn as much as possible, not to prove a point. Every new piece of information about the other side's needs is leverage.","Write three things you want to learn before your next negotiation."]],
q:[["What is 'mirroring'?",["Copying their posture","Repeating the last few words they said, as a question","Agreeing with everything","Looking in a mirror"],1,"It keeps them talking."],
["What does the late-night radio voice do?",["Shows anger","Signals calm control","Speeds things up","Shows excitement"],1,"Slow and downward-inflecting."],
["What should you do after a mirror or label?",["Explain your position","Stay silent","Make an offer","Change the subject"],1,"Silence invites them to say more."]],
x:["Explain mirroring and silence, and give an example.","Mirroring means repeating the last few words someone said, as a question. For example, if a supplier says 'We can't deliver until March because of staffing', you say 'Because of staffing?' and then stay silent. They usually explain more, which gives you information."]},
{t:"Tactical empathy",c:[
["Labelling","Name the other person's feelings: 'It seems like…', 'It sounds like…', 'It looks like…'. Naming a negative emotion tends to calm it; naming a positive one reinforces it. Don't say 'I'm hearing that…': keep the focus on them.","Use one label this week."],
["Accusation audit","Before they can, list the worst things they might be thinking about you: 'You're probably going to think this is unfair…' Saying it first takes the sting out and often makes them say 'No, it's not that bad.'","Write an accusation audit for a tough conversation."],
["Beware 'yes', master 'no'","'Yes' is often fake, given just to get rid of you. 'No' makes people feel safe and in control. Ask questions designed to get a 'no': 'Is now a bad time to talk?'","Rephrase a yes-question so the answer is 'no'."],
["The 'have you given up?' email","When someone goes quiet, Voss suggests a one-line email: 'Have you given up on this project?' It invites a 'no' and usually gets a reply.","Use it on one stalled conversation."],
["Aim for 'That's right'","Summarise the other side's view and feelings so well that they say 'That's right.' That's when they feel understood and start to move. 'You're right' usually means 'please stop talking'.","Summarise someone's position until they say 'That's right'."]],
q:[["How do you start a label?",["'I hear you…'","'It seems like…'","'I think you…'","'You should…'"],1,"Keep the focus on them."],
["What is an accusation audit?",["Checking someone's claims","Naming the negatives they might think about you before they do","An audit of contracts","Blaming them"],1,"It defuses objections."],
["Which phrase does Voss want to hear?",["'You're right'","'That's right'","'Maybe'","'Fine'"],1,"It means they feel understood."]],
x:["Write an accusation audit and a label for a real conversation you might have.","Example (asking a builder to redo work): 'You're probably going to think I'm being fussy and that this is a lot to ask so late on.' Then a label: 'It sounds like you've had a lot of changes on this job already.' Then listen."]},
{t:"Calibrated questions and bargaining",c:[
["Calibrated questions","Open questions starting with 'how' or 'what' make the other side think about your problem: 'How am I supposed to do that?', 'What's the biggest challenge you face?' Avoid 'why', which can sound accusatory.","Swap one 'why' question for a 'how' or 'what'."],
["The illusion of control","Calibrated questions give the other side a feeling of control while steering them towards your goal. They end up solving your problem.","Ask 'How can we make this work for both of us?'"],
["Guarantee execution","A 'yes' isn't a deal until it's done. Ask how it will be implemented: 'How will we know we're on track?' Voss suggests getting agreement three times in different ways to check it's real.","After your next agreement, ask 'What happens next?'"],
["The Ackerman model","For bargaining, set your target, then make offers at 65%, 85%, 95% and 100% of it, with shrinking increases. Use calibrated questions in between and end with a precise, non-round number (like £4,837), which feels carefully worked out. Add a non-monetary item at the end.","Plan Ackerman offers for something you're buying."],
["Black swans","Every negotiation has unknown unknowns: hidden facts that change everything. Find them by listening, asking and meeting face to face. They often explain why the other side is acting 'irrationally'.","Ask: what might they know that I don't?"]],
q:[["Which is a calibrated question?",["'Why did you do that?'","'Is this your best offer?'","'How am I supposed to do that?'","'Can you lower the price?'"],2,"How/what questions invite them to solve your problem."],
["In the Ackerman model, what's the first offer?",["50% of target","65% of target","85% of target","100% of target"],1,"Then 85%, 95% and 100%."],
["Why end on a precise number?",["It's easier to say","It feels carefully calculated","It's lucky","It's the law"],1,"Precise numbers feel credible."]],
x:["Plan a negotiation using Voss's tools, for example buying a used car.","Start with a calm voice and an accusation audit ('You probably get low-ballers all day'). Label feelings, mirror to get information, and ask calibrated questions ('How did you arrive at that price?'). Make Ackerman offers at 65%, 85%, 95% and a precise 100%, perhaps asking for something extra like new tyres at the end."]}]});
B({id:"common-sense-investing",title:"The Little Book of Common Sense Investing",author:"John C. Bogle",year:2007,topic:"Money",color:"#2b6b8a",
big:"Don't look for the needle in the haystack, buy the haystack: own the whole market at the lowest cost and hold it for decades.",
model:"Investors' net return = market return − costs. So: own the market (index funds), cut costs, ignore market timing, and stay the course.",
points:["Investors as a group earn the market's return, minus the costs they pay.","Long-term stock returns come from dividends and earnings growth; short-term swings come from changing moods.","Most active funds fall behind an index fund over long periods, after costs.","Past performance rarely persists: winners revert to the mean.","Costs compound against you: fees, trading, sales charges and taxes.","A low-cost index fund owns the whole haystack.","Balance shares and bonds to suit your age and nerves.","Stay the course: time in the market beats timing the market."],
s:[
{t:"The relentless rules of humble arithmetic",c:[
["The Gotrocks family","Bogle tells a parable of the Gotrocks family, who own every company in the market and share all the profits. Then 'Helpers' (brokers and managers) persuade family members to trade with each other to get ahead. The family's total return can only fall, because the Helpers take a cut.","Think of how many 'Helpers' take a cut from your investments."],
["Gross return minus costs","All investors together own the whole market, so before costs they earn the market's return. After costs, the average investor must earn less. This simple arithmetic is the heart of the book.","Find the yearly charge on one fund or pension you hold."],
["Where returns come from","Over the long run, share returns come from two sources: dividends and earnings growth (the investment return). In the short run, prices also swing with how much investors are willing to pay (the speculative return), which tends to even out over time.","Ask: am I investing in businesses or betting on moods?"],
["Buy the haystack","Instead of searching for the needle (the winning stock or fund), buy the haystack: a fund that owns the whole market. Bogle founded Vanguard and launched one of the first index funds for ordinary investors in the 1970s.","Check whether your pension offers a low-cost global tracker."],
["Simple beats complex","Bogle argues the winning strategy is simple: buy a diversified, low-cost portfolio of the whole market and hold it. Complexity usually benefits the seller, not the investor.","List any investment you hold that you couldn't explain in one sentence."]],
q:[["What does the Gotrocks parable show?",["Trading makes families rich","Helpers' fees reduce the group's total return","Stocks always rise","Bonds beat shares"],1,"Trading among themselves can't raise the total, but fees reduce it."],
["What does 'buy the haystack' mean?",["Buy farmland","Own the whole market through an index fund","Pick the best stock","Avoid shares"],1,"Don't search for the needle."],
["What drives long-term share returns?",["Market moods","Dividends and earnings growth","Interest rates only","Luck"],1,"The speculative part tends to even out."]],
x:["Explain Bogle's 'humble arithmetic' in your own words.","All investors together own the market, so on average they earn the market's return before costs. Costs then come off, so the average investor earns less than the market. The less you pay in costs, the closer you get to the market's return."]},
{t:"Costs and the fund-picking trap",c:[
["Costs compound too","A 1-2% yearly fee sounds small, but over 30-40 years it can take a large share of your final pot, because the money lost to fees would also have grown. Bogle calls this the tyranny of compounding costs.","Compare your fund's charge with a low-cost tracker."],
["All the costs","Costs include the annual management charge, trading costs inside the fund (from buying and selling), sales charges, platform fees and taxes. Many are hidden in the small print.","Look up your platform fee as well as fund charges."],
["Most active funds lose","Over long periods, most actively managed funds do worse than a simple index fund once their fees are taken off. The ones that win are hard to pick in advance.","Check how one of your funds did against its index over 10 years."],
["Reversion to the mean","Last decade's star fund rarely stays on top. Strong performance tends to fade back towards the average. Choosing funds by recent results often means buying high.","Avoid choosing a fund just for last year's returns."],
["Index funds and ETFs","Exchange-traded funds can be low-cost index funds too, but Bogle warns against trading them frequently. The benefit comes from owning and holding, not buying and selling.","Decide never to trade a tracker fund on news headlines."]],
q:[["Why do small fees matter so much?",["They're taxed","The lost money would also have compounded","They're paid once","They don't"],1,"Costs compound against you."],
["What is 'reversion to the mean' for funds?",["Top funds keep winning","Top performance tends to fade back to average","Funds merge","Fees rise"],1,"Past winners rarely stay winners."],
["What's Bogle's warning about ETFs?",["They're illegal","Don't trade them frequently","They're expensive","Only buy foreign ones"],1,"The benefit comes from holding."]],
x:["Why does Bogle say most investors should avoid picking funds by past performance?","Strong recent returns usually fade (reversion to the mean), and active funds charge higher fees that eat into returns. Over the long run most fall behind a low-cost index fund, and it's very hard to know in advance which few will win."]},
{t:"Building and keeping a simple portfolio",c:[
["Shares and bonds","Shares grow more over the long term but swing more. Bonds are steadier. Bogle suggested a rough starting guide of holding your age as a percentage in bonds, adjusted for your nerves and needs.","Write down your rough split of shares and bonds."],
["Diversify widely","Owning the whole market spreads your risk across thousands of companies, so no single failure can sink you. Bogle was a fan of the total US market; for UK investors, a global tracker does a similar job.","Check how many companies your portfolio really holds."],
["Don't time the market","Jumping in and out based on predictions usually means buying high and selling low. Time in the market matters more than timing the market.","Write down one rule that stops you selling in a panic."],
["Stay the course","Bogle's motto. Set a sensible plan, invest regularly, keep costs low and hold on through ups and downs. Doing nothing is often the hardest and best choice.","Set up a regular automatic investment."],
["Tax and wrappers","Costs include tax. For UK investors, using ISAs and pensions shelters growth from tax, which helps compounding. (This isn't financial advice: check your own situation.)","Check whether you're using your ISA and pension allowances."]],
q:[["What rough bond guide did Bogle suggest?",["None","Your age as a percentage in bonds","50% always","Only bonds after 70"],1,"A starting point, adjusted to you."],
["What's Bogle's motto?",["Buy low, sell high","Stay the course","Trade often","Follow the news"],1,"Patience and low costs win."],
["Why avoid market timing?",["It's illegal","It usually means buying high and selling low","It's too slow","It costs nothing"],1,"Time in the market beats timing."]],
x:["Describe a simple Bogle-style plan for a UK investor.","Choose a low-cost global index fund (and maybe a bond fund) inside an ISA or pension, set a share/bond split that suits your age and nerves, invest a fixed amount automatically each month, rebalance occasionally, and ignore headlines. (Not financial advice.)"]}]});

B({id:"grit",title:"Grit",author:"Angela Duckworth",year:2016,topic:"Mindset",color:"#7a6a1f",
big:"Passion and perseverance for long-term goals often matter more than talent, and grit can be grown.",
model:"Talent × effort = skill. Skill × effort = achievement. Grit grows from Interest → Practice → Purpose → Hope.",
points:["Grit is passion plus perseverance for long-term goals.","At West Point, grit predicted who got through basic training better than the entrance score.","We overvalue 'natural talent'.","Effort counts twice: it builds skill and turns skill into achievement.","Organise goals in a hierarchy, with one top-level goal as a compass.","Deliberate practice: stretch goals, full focus, quick feedback, reflection.","Purpose turns a job into a calling.","Grit grows with supportive-and-demanding parenting and gritty cultures."],
s:[
{t:"What grit is",c:[
["Beast Barracks","At West Point military academy, a tough summer training called 'Beast Barracks' makes some cadets drop out. Duckworth's short grit questionnaire predicted who'd stay better than the academy's own complicated entrance score.","Think of a time you stuck with something tough. What kept you going?"],
["Passion and perseverance","Grit is sticking with things over the long term: not just working hard, but working hard on the same top-level goal for years. Passion here means consistent interest, not burning intensity.","Name a goal you've been loyal to for years."],
["The talent bias","Studies show people often rate 'naturals' above 'strivers' even when the strivers did just as well. Duckworth argues our love of talent distracts us from effort.","Notice when you credit someone's success to 'talent'."],
["Effort counts twice","Talent × effort = skill. Skill × effort = achievement. Effort shows up in both steps, so someone with less talent but twice the effort can overtake a more talented person who stops.","Where could steady effort overtake raw talent for you?"],
["Grit grows","Grit tends to increase with age and experience. People learn that quitting has costs and that persistence pays. It isn't fixed.","Compare your grit now with ten years ago."]],
q:[["How does Duckworth define grit?",["Raw talent","Passion and perseverance for long-term goals","Physical toughness","Never asking for help"],1,"Sticking with long-term goals."],
["What did grit predict at West Point?",["Grades","Who would get through basic training","Height","Leadership"],1,"Better than the academy's entrance score."],
["Why does effort count twice?",["It's doubled in tests","It builds skill, then turns skill into achievement","It's twice as hard","It halves talent"],1,"Talent × effort = skill; skill × effort = achievement."]],
x:["Explain 'effort counts twice' with an example from your life.","Talent times effort gives skill, and skill times effort gives achievement. For example, in cycling, natural fitness plus regular training builds skill, and then consistently racing and training turns that skill into results. Someone who trains steadily can beat a more talented rider who doesn't."]},
{t:"Growing grit from the inside",c:[
["Interest","Passion usually starts with discovering something interesting, then developing it over time, then deepening it. Don't expect to 'find' a passion fully formed; follow curiosity and give it time.","What are you curious about that you could explore more?"],
["Goal hierarchy","Gritty people have a top-level goal that gives direction, with mid-level and low-level goals beneath it. Low-level goals can change; the top one stays. Duckworth describes this like a compass.","Write your top-level goal and two goals under it."],
["Deliberate practice","Experts practise differently: a clear stretch goal, full concentration, immediate feedback, then reflection and repetition until it's mastered. It's often hard and not much fun, and that's normal.","Plan one 20-minute deliberate practice session."],
["Flow and effort","Deliberate practice feels effortful; flow (being absorbed in something) feels effortless. Gritty people do both: hard practice to improve, and flow when performing.","Notice when you're practising versus performing."],
["Purpose","Duckworth tells the parable of three bricklayers: one is 'laying bricks', one is 'building a church', one is 'building the house of God'. Seeing how your work helps others turns a job into a calling and fuels persistence.","Write one line on who benefits from your work."]],
q:[["What's at the top of a gritty person's goal hierarchy?",["Many small goals","One top-level goal that gives direction","Daily to-dos","Other people's goals"],1,"It acts like a compass."],
["Which is part of deliberate practice?",["Repeating what you're good at","A stretch goal with immediate feedback","Practising only when motivated","Watching experts"],1,"Focus, feedback and reflection."],
["What does the bricklayer parable show?",["Building is hard","Purpose changes how you see the same work","Pay matters most","Teamwork"],1,"Job, career or calling."]],
x:["Describe your own goal hierarchy.","Top-level: a guiding aim (e.g. 'be a strong, healthy, capable dad who keeps learning'). Mid-level: specific goals that serve it (e.g. race fitness, a business idea). Low-level: daily actions (training sessions, reading). Lower goals can change as long as they serve the top one."]},
{t:"Hope and growing grit from the outside",c:[
["Hope","Grit depends on the hope that effort can improve things. Martin Seligman's research on 'learned helplessness' showed that feeling you have no control leads to giving up. Believing you can influence outcomes keeps you going.","Reframe one setback as temporary and specific."],
["Optimistic self-talk","Gritty people explain setbacks as temporary and specific ('I didn't prepare for that race') rather than permanent and pervasive ('I'm hopeless at racing'). This links to the growth mindset.","Rewrite a pessimistic thought in a temporary, specific way."],
["Wise parenting","Duckworth's research suggests children grow grittier with parents who are both supportive and demanding: warm, respectful, and holding high expectations.","How could you be both supportive and demanding with someone?"],
["The Hard Thing Rule","In Duckworth's family, everyone does a hard thing that needs deliberate practice. You can quit, but only at a natural stopping point, like the end of a season. Each person picks their own hard thing.","Pick your own 'hard thing'."],
["A culture of grit","Groups shape us. Joining a team or club where effort and persistence are normal makes you grittier too.","Name a group that raises your standards."]],
q:[["What did learned helplessness show?",["Practice always works","Believing you lack control leads to giving up","Rest is important","Talent wins"],1,"Hope that effort matters keeps people going."],
["What's the Hard Thing Rule?",["Never quit anything","Everyone does a hard thing and can only quit at a natural stopping point","Parents choose the hard thing","Only children do hard things"],1,"Choice plus commitment."],
["What style of parenting builds grit?",["Strict only","Relaxed only","Supportive and demanding","Hands-off"],2,"Warmth plus high expectations."]],
x:["How could you use the Hard Thing Rule in your family?","Each person (including you) picks a hard thing that needs regular practice, such as a sport, instrument or coding. You can quit, but only at a natural stopping point like the end of a term or season, not mid-way after a bad day. Parents model it by doing their own."]}]});

B({id:"obstacle-way",title:"The Obstacle Is the Way",author:"Ryan Holiday",year:2014,topic:"Mindset",color:"#4f4f4f",
big:"Every obstacle contains an opportunity: see it clearly, act on it persistently, and accept what you can't change.",
model:"Three disciplines from Stoicism: Perception (see clearly) → Action (act boldly and persistently) → Will (endure and accept).",
points:["What stands in the way becomes the way.","Control your perception: separate events from your judgement of them.","Focus on what's up to you.","Live in the present, one step at a time.","Act: get moving, persist, iterate and follow the process.","Look for the flank: indirect routes around obstacles.","Prepare for setbacks in advance (premeditatio malorum).","Love whatever happens (amor fati) and remember mortality."],
s:[
{t:"Perception",c:[
["The obstacle is the way","The Roman emperor and Stoic philosopher Marcus Aurelius wrote that what blocks action can advance it: what stands in the way becomes the way. Holiday builds the book on this idea.","Write down one current obstacle."],
["Steady your nerves","Holiday describes John D. Rockefeller as a young man staying calm and learning during the financial panic of 1857, while others panicked. Calm lets you see opportunities others miss.","Next time you feel panic, slow your breathing before you act."],
["Objectivity","The Stoics separated events from their judgement of them. Marcus described fine wine as 'fermented grape juice' to strip away the emotion. Describe a problem as if it were happening to someone else.","Describe your obstacle in plain, neutral words."],
["Is it up to you?","The Stoic philosopher Epictetus divided things into what is up to us (our judgements, choices and actions) and what isn't (other people, weather, the past). Put your energy only into the first.","Split a worry into 'up to me' and 'not up to me'."],
["Live in the present","Big problems feel overwhelming when you picture all of them at once. Focus on the next step, here and now.","What's the one step you can take today?"]],
q:[["What does 'the obstacle is the way' mean?",["Avoid obstacles","Working through obstacles is the path forward","Obstacles are bad luck","Wait for obstacles to pass"],1,"From Marcus Aurelius."],
["What did Epictetus divide things into?",["Good and bad","What's up to us and what isn't","Past and future","Mind and body"],1,"Put your energy into what's up to you."],
["Why describe a problem in neutral words?",["To ignore it","To remove emotion and see it clearly","To blame others","To make it sound worse"],1,"Objectivity helps you act well."]],
x:["Take one current problem and apply Stoic perception to it.","Describe it neutrally, as if it happened to someone else. Separate what's up to you (your effort, attitude, next action) from what isn't (others' choices, timing, luck). Then focus only on the next step that's within your control."]},
{t:"Action",c:[
["Get moving","Holiday's point is that perception alone isn't enough; you have to act. Start, even imperfectly. Momentum builds as you go.","Take one small action on your obstacle today."],
["Persist and iterate","Thomas Edison is said to have tested thousands of materials before finding a workable light-bulb filament. Each failure was information. Keep trying, and keep changing your approach based on what you learn.","Write down one thing you learned from your last failed attempt."],
["Follow the process","Instead of fixating on the end goal, focus on doing the next small thing well, then the next. The process breaks the impossible into the possible.","Break your goal into the next three steps."],
["The flank attack","If the direct route is blocked, look for an indirect one. Holiday points to Ulysses S. Grant's campaign at Vicksburg in the American Civil War, where repeated direct attacks failed until he went round the side.","List two indirect routes around your obstacle."],
["Use the obstacle against itself","Sometimes the obstacle can be turned to your advantage. Gandhi's non-violent resistance used the authorities' own force to win sympathy. Ask how the barrier could help you.","Ask: how could this obstacle work in my favour?"]],
q:[["What's the point of the Edison example?",["Inventors are lucky","Failures are information; keep iterating","Give up early","Work alone"],1,"Persistence plus learning."],
["What is a flank attack?",["A direct assault","Finding an indirect route around an obstacle","Giving up","Waiting"],1,"Grant at Vicksburg."],
["What does 'follow the process' mean?",["Only think about the goal","Focus on doing the next small step well","Copy others","Avoid planning"],1,"Break big into small."]],
x:["Plan an 'action' response to a real obstacle using Holiday's ideas.","Name the obstacle, the first small action you'll take now, how you'll iterate if it fails, and one indirect 'flank' route if the direct one stays blocked. Focus on the process (the next step), not the whole outcome."]},
{t:"Will",c:[
["The inner citadel","The Stoics talked of an inner fortress of calm that no outside event can break into. You build it in good times through practice and preparation, so it's there when things go wrong.","What builds your inner strength in good times?"],
["Premeditatio malorum","Stoics practised imagining what could go wrong before it happened. It isn't pessimism: it helps you prepare and makes setbacks less shocking.","Imagine three things that could go wrong this week and how you'd respond."],
["Amor fati: love your fate","In 1914 a fire destroyed much of Thomas Edison's factory. He reportedly called his family to watch it and then began rebuilding the next day. Accepting, even embracing, what happens frees you to move on.","Think of a setback you could learn to accept."],
["Something bigger than yourself","When obstacles feel personal, connecting to a larger purpose, like family, team or community, gives you strength to keep going.","Name who you're doing this for."],
["Memento mori","'Remember you will die.' The Stoics used this not to be gloomy, but to focus on what really matters and not waste time on trivia.","Ask: if I had limited time, would I still worry about this?"]],
q:[["What is premeditatio malorum?",["Ignoring risks","Imagining what could go wrong to prepare","Punishing mistakes","Planning revenge"],1,"Preparation, not pessimism."],
["What does amor fati mean?",["Fear of fate","Love of fate: embracing what happens","Fighting fate","Luck"],1,"Accept and use what happens."],
["Why practise memento mori?",["To be gloomy","To focus on what truly matters","To take more risks","To avoid planning"],1,"Time is limited, so don't waste it."]],
x:["How could perception, action and will help you with a challenge you face now?","Perception: see it clearly and neutrally and focus on what's up to you. Action: take the next step, iterate and look for indirect routes. Will: prepare for setbacks, accept what you can't change, and remember why it matters. A strong answer applies each to the same real challenge."]}]});

B({id:"coming-wave",title:"The Coming Wave",author:"Mustafa Suleyman",year:2023,topic:"AI",color:"#1f5a7a",
big:"AI and synthetic biology form a wave of technology that will be hard to contain; we need a 'narrow path' between catastrophe and dystopia.",
model:"Four features make the wave hard to contain: asymmetry, hyper-evolution, omni-use and autonomy. Containment needs many measures together, from technical safety and audits to international alliances.",
points:["Technologies spread in waves and get cheaper and more widespread.","Containment of past technologies has rarely worked.","The coming wave combines AI and synthetic biology.","Four features: asymmetry, hyper-evolution, omni-use, autonomy.","Powerful incentives drive it: competition, profit, research and ego.","The wave could weaken states through cyberattacks, misinformation and job disruption.","Avoid both catastrophe and an over-controlling surveillance state.","Containment needs many layers working together."],
s:[
{t:"Waves of technology",c:[
["Homo technologicus","Suleyman, a co-founder of DeepMind, argues humans have always been shaped by technology, from fire and the wheel to the printing press and electricity. Each general-purpose technology came as a wave that spread and changed society.","Name a technology that reshaped your parents' lives."],
["Proliferation","The historical pattern is that technologies get cheaper, easier to use and more widespread. Once they're useful, demand pulls them everywhere.","Think of a technology that went from rare to everywhere in your lifetime."],
["Containment rarely works","Suleyman looks at attempts to hold technologies back, such as the Ottoman Empire's long resistance to the printing press. In most cases they failed. Nuclear weapons are a partial exception, thanks to huge costs and treaties, but even that containment is imperfect.","Can you think of a technology that was successfully banned?"],
["The coming wave","The new wave combines AI and synthetic biology (engineering living things, helped by falling DNA-sequencing and synthesis costs and tools like CRISPR). Together they could transform medicine, energy, work and power.","Note one hope and one fear you have about this wave."],
["The pessimism-aversion trap","Suleyman says people, especially in tech, tend to look away from worrying possibilities because they're uncomfortable. He argues facing the risks squarely is the first step to managing them.","Ask what could go wrong with a technology you rely on."]],
q:[["What does history suggest about containing technology?",["It usually works","It has rarely worked","It's always easy","Only governments try"],1,"Nuclear weapons are a partial exception."],
["What two technologies make up the coming wave?",["Crypto and robotics","AI and synthetic biology","5G and drones","Nuclear and solar"],1,"Together they could transform society."],
["What is the pessimism-aversion trap?",["Being too negative","Looking away from uncomfortable risks","Avoiding pessimists","Refusing to invest"],1,"Ignoring risks makes them harder to manage."]],
x:["Explain why Suleyman thinks containing this wave will be hard.","History shows technologies spread, get cheaper and are rarely contained. This wave is especially hard because it's cheap, fast-moving, useful for almost everything and increasingly autonomous, and because strong incentives push everyone to develop it."]},
{t:"Why this wave is different",c:[
["Asymmetry","The wave gives huge power to small groups or even individuals. A single person with AI or biotech tools could have outsized impact, for good or harm.","Think of a way one person could use AI for great good."],
["Hyper-evolution","Progress is happening extremely fast, with each advance speeding up the next. Rules and institutions struggle to keep pace.","Notice how fast AI tools you use have changed in a year."],
["Omni-use","These technologies are general-purpose: the same tool can design medicines or toxins, write code or write malware. That makes it hard to allow the good uses while blocking the bad.","Name one AI tool with both good and bad uses."],
["Autonomy","AI systems are increasingly able to act on their own, setting sub-goals and taking actions without step-by-step human control. That raises new questions about oversight.","Where would you want a human to stay in control?"],
["Unstoppable incentives","Suleyman lists powerful drivers: competition between nations, the openness of research, huge profits and personal ambition. No single actor can simply stop.","Which incentive do you think is strongest?"]],
q:[["What does 'omni-use' mean?",["Only used by governments","The same technology can be used for many purposes, good and bad","Used everywhere at once","Free to use"],1,"Hard to permit good uses while blocking bad ones."],
["What does 'asymmetry' describe?",["Unequal pay","Small groups gaining outsized power","Lopsided designs","Slow progress"],1,"One person can have huge impact."],
["Why can't one actor simply stop the wave?",["It's illegal","Strong incentives like competition and profit drive everyone","It's already finished","There's no demand"],1,"Incentives push development forward."]],
x:["Choose one of the four features (asymmetry, hyper-evolution, omni-use, autonomy) and explain it with an example.","Example, omni-use: an AI model that helps scientists design new medicines could also be misused to design harmful substances. Because the same capability serves both, it's hard to allow the benefits while blocking the risks."]},
{t:"States, risks and the narrow path",c:[
["Fragile states","Suleyman argues the wave could weaken nation-states: cyberattacks on hospitals and infrastructure, AI-generated misinformation, autonomous weapons and job disruption all strain the institutions that keep us safe.","Which public service would you most want protected?"],
["Catastrophe or dystopia","He describes a dilemma. Too little control risks catastrophe from misuse or accidents. Too much control risks dystopia: a surveillance state that watches everyone to prevent harm. We need a narrow path between the two.","Where would you draw the line on surveillance for safety?"],
["Layers of containment","Suleyman proposes ten steps that must work together: technical safety research, audits, using choke points (like advanced chips), responsible builders, businesses that balance profit and purpose, stronger governments, international alliances, a culture that learns from failure, public movements, and keeping all of them in balance.","Which step do you think matters most?"],
["Learning from aviation","He points to aviation as a model: an industry that shares information about failures openly so everyone gets safer. AI needs a similar culture.","Think of an industry you trust because of its safety record."],
["A modern Turing test","Suleyman suggests a new test of AI capability: could an AI, given $100,000, turn it into $1 million by planning and acting on its own? It reflects the shift from AI that talks to AI that does.","What tasks would you be comfortable letting an AI agent do for you?"]],
q:[["What two bad outcomes does Suleyman want to avoid?",["Boom and bust","Catastrophe and dystopia","War and peace","Inflation and deflation"],1,"The narrow path runs between them."],
["Why does he point to aviation?",["Planes use AI","It openly shares failures to improve safety","It's heavily taxed","It's slow to change"],1,"A model safety culture."],
["What does his 'modern Turing test' ask?",["Can AI chat like a human?","Can AI turn $100,000 into $1 million on its own?","Can AI pass exams?","Can AI write poetry?"],1,"From AI that talks to AI that acts."]],
x:["In your view, which containment step matters most, and why?","A good answer picks one of the steps (e.g. technical safety, audits, international alliances, chip choke points) and explains why, with a concrete example, while noting Suleyman's point that no single step works alone."]}]});
